.\tools\pipeline\matrix.html:15613:*** AND THE FIRST FORM OF THAT DERIVER WAS KEYED ON A CORRELATE, WHICH I CHECKED BEFORE TRUSTING IT. ***  `^#+ .*#NNN\b` tests the LITERAL number, while the class is *is this round documented* -- and a RANGE heading (`## #89-#94`, which this file really does use) reports 0 for a round it documents.  Checked: no range heading covers any of the seven, and the bracketing headings confirm each gap (R9=#435, R10=#437 straddle #436; R12=#440, R13=#442 straddle #441).  *The correlate would have produced a plausible wrong answer in the direction that manufactures a finding.* — evidence: regress/artifacts/recordsaudit_20260917/RECORDS_grok_20260917.txt; CHANGELOG.md; git log --all --format=&#x27;%h|%s&#x27;</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">evdeadarchive</span><span class="ttl">297 ev citations across 27 rows name tools/pipeline/evidence/, a directory that was never tracked in any commit -- the answers survive in the panel dirs, so the remedy is a re-point, not a recovery</span><span class="tip">297 ev citations across 27 rows name tools/pipeline/evidence/, a directory that was never tracked in any commit -- the answers survive in the panel dirs, so the remedy is a re-point, not a recovery — note: *** 297 `ev` CITATIONS NAME `tools/pipeline/evidence/`, AND THAT DIRECTORY WAS NEVER BUILT -- NOT DELETED, NEVER TRACKED.  Measured 2026-09-17. ***  `git log --all -- &#x27;tools/pipeline/evidence&#x27;` returns **0 commits**, and `--diff-filter=A` over `*evidence*` returns **nothing ever added to the index**.  So these are not decayed citations; they are citations to an ARCHIVE SCHEME THAT WAS PRESCRIBED AND NEVER IMPLEMENTED -- the PIPELINE.md doctrine&#x27;s layout, written into rows at its 2026-08-15 adoption (`pipeline/evidence/d1/assess/codex_2026-08-15.md` and siblings). **27 distinct rows**: a1a2 a4 b101 b115 b118 b120 b122 b126 b1w b27 b47 b75 b82 d1 devexit gateflr hlen n1 n2 n3 n4 n5 n6 pkbrief + 3 more. *** THE ANSWERS SURVIVE, WHICH CHANGES THE DISPOSITION FROM RECOVERY TO RE-POINT. ***  `_scratchpad/panel_20260815_*` holds ten panel dirs for that date, each carrying `agy.txt codex.txt grok.txt kimi.txt ollama_{deepseek,glm,minimax}.txt`.  **The seat answers were written; only the path they are cited at is fictional.**  A round closing this re-points the 297 fields at the real panel dirs (and, per the durable-path rule, copies them under `regress/artifacts/` -- `_scratchpad/` is itself volatile). **SCALE, DERIVED BY WALKING THE DECODED LEDGER RATHER THAN BY GREP:** **3222 `ev` fields** (row-level + entry-level), 3209 non-empty; **1923 cite `_scratchpad/`**; 297 cite the dead archive; 989 cite neither. *** AND THE AUDIT&#x27;S OWN NUMBERS WERE TRUNCATED GREPS THAT SAID SO -- WHICH IS WHY THEY WERE RE-DERIVED AND NOT TRANSCRIBED. ***  It reported `&gt;=1999` ev fields and `&gt;=999` _scratchpad citations, flagging both as cut off, and gave 297 as COMPLETE.  The walk reproduces **297 exactly** and shows the two lower bounds were low by ~1.6x and ~1.9x.  *An honest lower bound is a good record and a bad number: it is indistinguishable from a measurement once it is quoted one hop away from the tool that produced it*, which is this project&#x27;s frozen-number class arriving through the seat rather than through the author. **WHY NO CHECKER SEES IT, and it is a scope boundary rather than a checker defect.**  `check_cited_artifacts.py` exists and does exactly this job -- but its own header sets the altitude deliberately: *&quot;checks committed records, never scratchpad state&quot;*, per the fable5 `#462` Decision 4c.  It reads CHANGELOG ROUND BLOCKS, not ledger `ev` fields.  So the 297 are outside it BY DESIGN, and widening it is a scope change the adjudication that created it explicitly bounded. **RANKED LOW, and the reason is the same one the carrier&#x27;s own citation-form ruling gives: NO GATE, PROBE OR SCRIPT READS AN `ev` FIELD FUNCTIONALLY.**  It is consumed by a reader, and a reader following a `pipeline/evidence/...` path gets an immediate, loud failure -- not a wrong answer.  That is the SAFE direction.  The cost is audit expense and the fact that 27 rows cannot be verified from their own fields.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><