27;S OWN NUMBERS WERE TRUNCATED GREPS THAT SAID SO -- WHICH IS WHY THEY WERE RE-DERIVED AND NOT TRANSCRIBED. ***  It reported `&gt;=1999` ev fields and `&gt;=999` _scratchpad citations, flagging both as cut off, and gave 297 as COMPLETE.  The walk reproduces **297 exactly** and shows the two lower bounds were low by ~1.6x and ~1.9x.  *An honest lower bound is a good record and a bad number: it is indistinguishable from a measurement once it is quoted one hop away from the tool that produced it*, which is this project&#x27;s frozen-number class arriving through the seat rather than through the author. **WHY NO CHECKER SEES IT, and it is a scope boundary rather than a checker defect.**  `check_cited_artifacts.py` exists and does exactly this job -- but its own header sets the altitude deliberately: *&quot;checks committed records, never scratchpad state&quot;*, per the fable5 `#462` Decision 4c.  It reads CHANGELOG ROUND BLOCKS, not ledger `ev` fields.  So the 297 are outside it BY DESIGN, and widening it is a scope change the adjudication that created it explicitly bounded. **RANKED LOW, and the reason is the same one the carrier&#x27;s own citation-form ruling gives: NO GATE, PROBE OR SCRIPT READS AN `ev` FIELD FUNCTIONALLY.**  It is consumed by a reader, and a reader following a `pipeline/evidence/...` path gets an immediate, loud failure -- not a wrong answer.  That is the SAFE direction.  The cost is audit expense and the fact that 27 rows cannot be verified from their own fields. — evidence: regress/artifacts/recordsaudit_20260917/RECORDS_grok_20260917.txt; git log --all -- tools/pipeline/evidence (0 commits); _scratchpad/panel_20260815_202720/</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ledgercommitsshape</span><span class="ttl">ledger `commits` is a space-separated string in 382 rows and a list in 37, so a consumer that assumes a list silently yields CHARACTERS and renders one SHA as seven commits</span><span class="tip">ledger `commits` is a space-separated string in 382 rows and a list in 37, so a consumer that assumes a list silently yields CHARACTERS and renders one SHA as seven commits — note: *** THE LEDGER&#x27;S `commits` FIELD IS A SPACE-SEPARATED STRING IN 382 ROWS AND A LIST IN 37, AND A CONSUMER THAT ASSUMES A LIST FAILS SILENTLY BY YIELDING CHARACTERS.  Measured 2026-09-17 -- by falling into it. ***  `&quot;, &quot;.join(row[&quot;commits&quot;])` on a string row renders `7a41b3f` as `7, a, 4, 1, b, 3, f`, which **looks like a list of seven commits** rather than like an error.  Census over 512 rows: `str` 382, `NoneType` 93, `list` 37; 132 of the strings are non-empty. **IT IS NOT A DATA DEFECT -- both shapes are parseable and the shipped tools handle them** (`gen_dashboard.py` and the checkers all work today).  It is a SCHEMA INCONSISTENCY that makes every new consumer a coin-flip, and the failure mode is the one this project keeps naming: **a plausible wrong answer that does not look like a failure.**  The records audit&#x27;s own Q1 had to count &quot;hash-bearing rows&quot; precisely because the field has two shapes. **DISPOSITION: FILED, NOT NORMALISED.**  Rewriting 382 rows to lists is a mechanical edit to the single source of truth with no measured defect behind it -- the ADDITION LICENCE refuses &quot;cannot hurt&quot;, and this is its records-side twin.  The cheap durable fix is a one-line accessor (`commits_of(row)` returning a list from either shape) adopted where consumers are written, which is FILED as a tooling change owing its own negative controls. *Recorded because the near-miss is the instructive part: the wrong output was nine rows of single letters in a brief-building extraction, and it was obvious ONLY because a SHA is recognisable.  A field of short tokens would have rendered the same way and read as real.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE LEDGER&#x27;S `commits` FIELD IS A SPACE-SEPARATED STRING IN 382 ROWS AND A LIST IN 37, AND A CONSUMER THAT ASSUMES A LIST FAILS SILENTLY BY YIELDING CHARACTERS.  Measured 2026-09-17 -- by falling into it. ***  `&quot;, &quot;.join(row[&quot;commits&quot;])` on a string row renders `7a41b3f` as `7, a, 4, 1, b, 3, f`, which **looks like a list of seven commits** rather than like an error.  Census over 512 rows: `str` 382, `NoneType` 93, `list` 37; 132 of the strings are non-empty. **IT IS NOT A DATA DEFECT -- both shapes are parseable and the shipped tools handle them** (`gen_dashboard.py` and the checkers all work today).  It is a SCHEMA INCONSISTENCY that makes every new consumer a coin-flip, and the failure mode is the one this project keeps naming: **a plausible wrong answer that does not look like a failure.**  The records audit&#x27;s own Q1 had to count &quot;hash-bearing rows&quot; precisely because the field has two shapes. **DISPOSITION: FILED, NOT NORMALISED.**  Rewriting 382 rows to lists is a mechanical edit to the single source of truth with no measured defect behind it -- the ADDITION LICENCE refuses &quot;cannot hurt&quot;, and this is its records-side twin.  The cheap durable fix is a one-line accessor (`commits_of(row)` returning a list from either shape) adopted where consumers are written, which is FILED as a tooling change owing its own negative controls. *Recorded because the near-miss is the instructive part: the wrong output was nine rows of single letters in a brief-building extraction, and it was obvious ONLY because a SHA is recognisable.  A field of short tokens would have rendered the same way and read as real.* — evidence: tools/pipeline/ledger.json -- type census: str 382, NoneType 93, list 37</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4dmacnote</span><span class="ttl">sh4_dmac_transfer signals transfer end through no channel -- it never sets CHCR_TE nor clears DE, while dev_pvr.c does; a guest polling CHCR on the generic DMAC path would never see completion, IF such a path transfers</span><span class="tip">sh4_dmac_transfer signals transfer end through no channel -- it never sets CHCR_TE nor clears DE, while dev_pvr.c does; a guest polling CHCR on the generic DMAC path would never see completion, IF such a path transfers — note: *** THE COMMENT IS WRONG; WHETHER THE BEHAVIOUR IS WRONG IS **NOT** MEASURED, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE ROW. ***  `sh4_dmac_transfer` never sets `CHCR_TE` and never clears `CHCR_DE`, while `dev_pvr.c` sets TE on its own DMA path.  A guest that polls CHCR for transfer end on the GENERIC DMAC path would therefore never see it. **WHY THIS IS FILED AND NOT CLAIMED:** `#453`&#x27;s own analysis records that the only RS encoding reaching this point is the SINGLE-ADDRESS EXTERNAL case, **which performs no transfer** -- so &quot;never signals completion&quot; may be exactly right for the one path that arrives.  *Not signalling the end of a transfer that did not happen is not a defect.* **WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT, and it is the ordinary ladder question:** a rung-3 probe that drives the generic DMAC on a path which DOES transfer, then reads CHCR back through `DEVICE_ACCESS` and asks whether TE is set.  If no such path is reachable, the honest answer is that the arm is unreachable and the row closes as documented-only -- which is a real answer, not a failure. *Ranked LOW: it is a HANG-class candidate (the third defect class this queue tracks) with no witness and a named reason to think it may be vacuous.  It ranks below every measured defect and is recorded so the question is not lost a second time.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — BELIEVED — *** THE COMMENT IS WRONG; WHETHER THE BEHAVIOUR IS WRONG IS **NOT** MEASURED, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE ROW. ***  `sh4_dmac_transfer` never sets `CHCR_TE` and never clears `CHCR_DE`, while `dev_pvr.c` sets TE on its own DMA path.  A guest that polls CHCR for transfer end on the GENERIC DMAC path would therefore never see it. **WHY THIS IS FILED AND NOT CLAIMED:** `#453`&#x27;s own analysis records that the only RS encoding reaching this point is the SINGLE-ADDRESS EXTERNAL case, **which performs no transfer** -- so &quot;never signals completion&quot; may be exactly right for the one path that arrives.  *Not signalling the end of a transfer that did not happen is not a defect.* **WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT, and it is the ordinary ladder question:** a rung-3 probe that drives the generic DMAC on a path which DOES transfer, then reads CHCR back through `DEVICE_ACCESS` and asks whether TE is set.  If no such path is reachable, the honest answer is that the arm is unreachable and the row closes as documented-only -- which is a real answer, not a failure. *Ranked LOW: it is a HANG-class candidate (the third defect class this queue tracks) with no witness and a named reason to think it may be vacuous.  It ranks below every measured defect and is recorded so the question is not lost a second time.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/grok.txt; src/devices/dev_sh4.c; src/devices/dev_pvr.c</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromdmaflood</span><span class="ttl">dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c prints an UNLATCHED guest-drivable fatal() on the ACCEPTED-DMA path, so ordinary correct guest traffic floods the log and -q cannot silence it -- a floodclass site the floodclass row does not name</span><span class="tip">dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c prints an UNLATCHED guest-drivable fatal() on the ACCEPTED-DMA path, so ordinary correct guest traffic floods the log and -q cannot silence it -- a floodclass site the floodclass row does not name — note: *** AN UNLATCHED GUEST-DRIVABLE `fatal()` ON THE **SUCCESS** PATH -- IT FIRES ON EVERY ACCEPTED DMA, NOT ON A REFUSAL.  Verified 2026-09-17 by brace depth, not by eye. ***  `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` prints `[ dreamcast_gdrom_dma: Transfering %i bytes to 0x%08x (guest asked for %i) ]` on the accepted-transfer path with **no latch of any kind**. **HOW IT WAS SETTLED, because the obvious check is misleading here:** the enclosing function DOES contain a latch -- `if (!(d-&gt;warned &amp; GDROM_W_DMANODATA))` -- about thirty lines above the print, so a reader scanning for *&quot;is there a `d-&gt;warned` test nearby&quot;* finds one and stops.  **Brace depth settles it: that block CLOSES before the print**, which sits outside it (depth -1 relative to the latch&#x27;s `if`).  *A latch in the same function is not a latch on this line, and the two are indistinguishable without counting braces.* **WHY THE SUCCESS PATH IS THE BAD PLACE FOR IT.**  Every other flood this queue tracks is on a REFUSAL arm, where the guest has to do something unusual to provoke it.  This one is on the path a working driver takes, so **ordinary correct guest behaviour produces the flood** -- and `fatal()` has no `-q` early-out, so it cannot be silenced by verbosity. **NOT INTRODUCED BY `#462`, AND THE SEAT SAID SO ITSELF** (*&quot;This is not introduced by the diff -- the original also had it&quot;*).  `#462` MOVED the print below the `d-&gt;data == NULL` check and changed it to print `d-&gt;data_len` rather than the guest&#x27;s register, which is why the line is correct about its CONTENT and still unlatched about its FREQUENCY. *** `floodclass` DOES NOT COVER THIS SITE: checked, and the row mentions neither `gdrom`, `dreamcast` nor `Transfering`. ***  So it is a genuinely new member of a class this project already tracks, rather than a duplicate. **DISPOSITION IS NOT OBVIOUS AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHOSEN HERE.**  The house idiom is member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE -- but this is not a decline, it is a SUCCESS, and a `(once)` latch on a success path silences the transfer log after the first DMA, which may be the diagnostic someone wants.  The option set for a NOISY-BUT-CORRECT print is not the de-fatalisation set, and naming the right menu is the first job of whoever takes this. *Provenance: the deepseek seat, `panel_20260917_100431`, in an answer STAMPED ACROSS SIX ROWS -- so the finding had no cell of its own.  Third real defect recovered today from a review that was written, delivered and never read as its own answer.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** AN UNLATCHED GUEST-DRIVABLE `fatal()` ON THE **SUCCESS** PATH -- IT FIRES ON EVERY ACCEPTED DMA, NOT ON A REFUSAL.  Verified 2026-09-17 by brace depth, not by eye. ***  `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` prints `[ dreamcast_gdrom_dma: Transfering %i bytes to 0x%08x (guest asked for %i) ]` on the accepted-transfer path with **no latch of any kind**. **HOW IT WAS SETTLED, because the obvious check is misleading here:** the enclosing function DOES contain a latch -- `if (!(d-&gt;warned &amp; GDROM_W_DMANODATA))` -- about thirty lines above the print, so a reader scanning for *&quot;is there a `d-&gt;warned` test nearby&quot;* finds one and stops.  **Brace depth settles it: that block CLOSES before the print**, which sits outside it (depth -1 relative to the latch&#x27;s `if`).  *A latch in the same function is not a latch on this line, and the two are indistinguishable without counting braces.* **WHY THE SUCCESS PATH IS THE BAD PLACE FOR IT.**  Every other flood this queue tracks is on a REFUSAL arm, where the guest has to do something unusual to provoke it.  This one is on the path a working driver takes, so **ordinary correct guest behaviour produces the flood** -- and `fatal()` has no `-q` early-out, so it cannot be silenced by verbosity. **NOT INTRODUCED BY `#462`, AND THE SEAT SAID SO ITSELF** (*&quot;This is not introduced by the diff -- the original also had it&quot;*).  `#462` MOVED the print below the `d-&gt;data == NULL` check and changed it to print `d-&gt;data_len` rather than the guest&#x27;s register, which is why the line is correct about its CONTENT and still unlatched about its FREQUENCY. *** `floodclass` DOES NOT COVER THIS SITE: checked, and the row mentions neither `gdrom`, `dreamcast` nor `Transfering`. ***  So it is a genuinely new member of a class this project already tracks, rather than a duplicate. **DISPOSITION IS NOT OBVIOUS AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHOSEN HERE.**  The house idiom is member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE -- but this is not a decline, it is a SUCCESS, and a `(once)` latch on a success path silences the transfer log after the first DMA, which may be the diagnostic someone wants.  The option set for a NOISY-BUT-CORRECT print is not the de-fatalisation set, and naming the right menu is the first job of whoever takes this. *Provenance: the deepseek seat, `panel_20260917_100431`, in an answer STAMPED ACROSS SIX ROWS -- so the finding had no cell of its own.  Third real defect recovered today from a review that was written, delivered and never read as its own answer.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_deepseek.txt; src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c -- the `Transfering&#x27; fatal, measured OUTSIDE the GDROM_W_DMANODATA block by brace depth</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wallverdictand</span><span class="ttl">seat_verdicts.py suppresses a genuine wall when the seat streamed a verdict-shaped phrase -- `and not v` applies the WEAK-pattern condition to STRONG ones too, against the file&#x27;s own documented rule; 2 of 166 walls hidden</span><span class="tip">seat_verdicts.py suppresses a genuine wall when the seat streamed a verdict-shaped phrase -- `and not v` applies the WEAK-pattern condition to STRONG ones too, against the file&#x27;s own documented rule; 2 of 166 walls hidden — note: *** `seat_verdicts.py` CAN SCORE A GENUINE WALL AS `answered` WHEN THE SEAT STREAMED ENOUGH TEXT TO YIELD A VERDICT-SHAPED PHRASE.  Measured 2026-09-17. ***  `is_walled = _is_wall(t[-600:], t[-4000:]) and not v`.  The tail test is right and fires; `and not v` suppresses it. **IT CONTRADICTS THE FILE&#x27;S OWN DOCUMENTED RULE**, which distinguishes *&quot;STRONG ones are a notice on their own; WEAK ones (`quota`, `rate limit`) need&quot;* the extra condition -- and then applies the extra condition to both.  `usage limit` and `quota will reset` are both in `WALL_STRONG`. **MEASURED POPULATION, not generalised from the instance:** 2,369 seat files / 356 panel dirs; **166 walls by the tail test; 2 hidden** -- `panel_20260917_085545/kimi.txt` (2.4 MB, today, on the in-flight `#530`) and `panel_20260820_005715/codex.txt` (21 KB). **WHY THE GUARD EXISTS, so nobody removes it blindly:** the file records that the wall test twice fired on LIVE seats that were merely DISCUSSING rate limits.  The fix for that was the TAIL RESTRICTION (`t[-600:]`), which is the real discriminator; `and not v` is belt-and-braces that now costs more than it buys for STRONG patterns. **THE SHAPE OF A FIX, filed rather than applied:** report WALLED on a STRONG pattern in the tail regardless of `v`, and keep `and not v` for WEAK patterns only -- which is exactly what the file already says it does.  It owes two negative controls (a live seat discussing quotas must NOT be walled; a strong-notice tail with a verdict in the stream MUST be) and it is a third checker change in one session, so it ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** `seat_verdicts.py` CAN SCORE A GENUINE WALL AS `answered` WHEN THE SEAT STREAMED ENOUGH TEXT TO YIELD A VERDICT-SHAPED PHRASE.  Measured 2026-09-17. ***  `is_walled = _is_wall(t[-600:], t[-4000:]) and not v`.  The tail test is right and fires; `and not v` suppresses it. **IT CONTRADICTS THE FILE&#x27;S OWN DOCUMENTED RULE**, which distinguishes *&quot;STRONG ones are a notice on their own; WEAK ones (`quota`, `rate limit`) need&quot;* the extra condition -- and then applies the extra condition to both.  `usage limit` and `quota will reset` are both in `WALL_STRONG`. **MEASURED POPULATION, not generalised from the instance:** 2,369 seat files / 356 panel dirs; **166 walls by the tail test; 2 hidden** -- `panel_20260917_085545/kimi.txt` (2.4 MB, today, on the in-flight `#530`) and `panel_20260820_005715/codex.txt` (21 KB). **WHY THE GUARD EXISTS, so nobody removes it blindly:** the file records that the wall test twice fired on LIVE seats that were merely DISCUSSING rate limits.  The fix for that was the TAIL RESTRICTION (`t[-600:]`), which is the real discriminator; `and not v` is belt-and-braces that now costs more than it buys for STRONG patterns. **THE SHAPE OF A FIX, filed rather than applied:** report WALLED on a STRONG pattern in the tail regardless of `v`, and keep `and not v` for WEAK patterns only -- which is exactly what the file already says it does.  It owes two negative controls (a live seat discussing quotas must NOT be walled; a strong-notice tail with a verdict in the stream MUST be) and it is a third checker change in one session, so it ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider. — evidence: tools/pipeline/seat_verdicts.py; 2369-file wall audit over 356 panel dirs</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">cellechoesbrief</span><span class="ttl">ten ledger cells recorded the BRIEF back as the seat&#x27;s answer -- a substring match on CLOSE fired on `closed` in the brief&#x27;s headings -- and section G cannot see it, because an entry exists</span><span class="tip">ten ledger cells recorded the BRIEF back as the seat&#x27;s answer -- a substring match on CLOSE fired on `closed` in the brief&#x27;s headings -- and section G cannot see it, because an entry exists — note: *** TEN OF SIXTY-THREE LEDGER CELLS RECORDED THE BRIEF BACK AS THE SEAT&#x27;S ANSWER, AND NO GATE CAN SEE IT.  Found by the Fable seat 2026-09-17; measured and repaired the same day. ***  **ROOT CAUSE, and it is one character class:** the extraction matched `CLOSE|KEEP-OPEN|NOT CHECKABLE` as a SUBSTRING, so **`CLOSE` fired on the word `closed`** -- which appears in the brief&#x27;s own headings (`### 1. \`lcashiftub\` (LOW, closed) -- ...`) and in seat narration (*&quot;I&#x27;ll verify each closed row&quot;*).  It then took the FIRST matching line, which for a seat that ECHOES the brief (codex) or NARRATES ITS PLAN (grok) is not its verdict. **MEASURED: 9 codex cells were brief headings; 1 grok cell was a 926-character opening narration.  53 of 63 were genuine.**  All ten repaired -- codex&#x27;s from its real per-row verdict table, grok&#x27;s from its row-9 table line. *** WHY IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNREAD REVIEW, in the Fable seat&#x27;s words: &quot;an unread review filed under a RECORDED seat -- section G cannot see it because an entry exists.&quot; ***  Section G&#x27;s whole question is *did a seat that answered get recorded*; a cell containing the BRIEF answers yes.  **The blank that would have been caught was filled with something shaped like an answer** -- the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement family, one step further along than any instance this project has recorded. **AND MY OWN AUDIT OF IT UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of the session, committed inside the audit of the fifth -- which is why the repair was done by RE-EXTRACTING ALL 63 with a corrected key and DIFFING, rather than by patching the rows the audit named.* **THE STRUCTURAL LANDING SPOT, FILED NOT BUILT:** a checker that flags a ledger cell whose text appears verbatim in the `brief_sent.md` of the panel dir the entry cites.  Both files are already on disk and already linked by the `ev` field, so the comparison is cheap and needs no new convention.  It owes two negative controls (a genuine cell quoting a short phrase from the brief must NOT fire; a pasted heading MUST) and is a tooling change, so it ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** TEN OF SIXTY-THREE LEDGER CELLS RECORDED THE BRIEF BACK AS THE SEAT&#x27;S ANSWER, AND NO GATE CAN SEE IT.  Found by the Fable seat 2026-09-17; measured and repaired the same day. ***  **ROOT CAUSE, and it is one character class:** the extraction matched `CLOSE|KEEP-OPEN|NOT CHECKABLE` as a SUBSTRING, so **`CLOSE` fired on the word `closed`** -- which appears in the brief&#x27;s own headings (`### 1. \`lcashiftub\` (LOW, closed) -- ...`) and in seat narration (*&quot;I&#x27;ll verify each closed row&quot;*).  It then took the FIRST matching line, which for a seat that ECHOES the brief (codex) or NARRATES ITS PLAN (grok) is not its verdict. **MEASURED: 9 codex cells were brief headings; 1 grok cell was a 926-character opening narration.  53 of 63 were genuine.**  All ten repaired -- codex&#x27;s from its real per-row verdict table, grok&#x27;s from its row-9 table line. *** WHY IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNREAD REVIEW, in the Fable seat&#x27;s words: &quot;an unread review filed under a RECORDED seat -- section G cannot see it because an entry exists.&quot; ***  Section G&#x27;s whole question is *did a seat that answered get recorded*; a cell containing the BRIEF answers yes.  **The blank that would have been caught was filled with something shaped like an answer** -- the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement family, one step further along than any instance this project has recorded. **AND MY OWN AUDIT OF IT UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of the session, committed inside the audit of the fifth -- which is why the repair was done by RE-EXTRACTING ALL 63 with a corrected key and DIFFING, rather than by patching the rows the audit named.* **THE STRUCTURAL LANDING SPOT, FILED NOT BUILT:** a checker that flags a ledger cell whose text appears verbatim in the `brief_sent.md` of the panel dir the entry cites.  Both files are already on disk and already linked by the `ev` field, so the comparison is cheap and needs no new convention.  It owes two negative controls (a genuine cell quoting a short phrase from the brief must NOT fire; a pasted heading MUST) and is a tooling change, so it ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/codex.txt _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/grok.txt vs the recorded cells; fable adjudication 2026-09-17</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ip30asdvasdvnb</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_ip30.c DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_ip30_2) case 0x114+0x40*(12-8) carries an UNLATCHED guest-drivable fatal() whose message is a keyboard mash (asdvasdvnb), on an arm that is actually implemented and returns the documented 0x80000000 alive value</span><span class="tip">dev_sgi_ip30.c DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_ip30_2) case 0x114+0x40*(12-8) carries an UNLATCHED guest-drivable fatal() whose message is a keyboard mash (asdvasdvnb), on an arm that is actually implemented and returns the documented 0x80000000 alive value — note: FILED 2026-09-17 by the #531 pass-2 grok seat; every element verified here before filing.  NOT one of #531&#x27;s four sites -- it carries no abort() and no exit(, so it kills nothing.  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, and a second joins only in the SAME semantic class; a host kill and a log flood are different classes, which is why this is filed rather than folded in.  UPSTREAM RESIDUE, checked against HEAD rather than assumed: present before #531 and never touched by the fork.  TWO defects in one line -- UNLATCHED (every guest read prints; floodclass already records guest-drivable log floods as a CLASS, and the house idiom is N=1 per device instance with an unqualified &#x27;(once)&#x27; marker) and a MEANINGLESS MESSAGE that tells a reader nothing.  DISPOSITION when it is taken: member 1 DELETE, not member 3.  The arm computes the right value and refuses nothing, so there is no event to announce, and a bounded announce would ship a wrong record in product code -- exactly what the footbridge +0x188 case established.  WITNESS when it is taken: a rung-3 probe reading that offset and counting emissions.  The #531 probe&#x27;s vehicle already reaches this device, so the cost is one session, not a new rig.  *** WARRANT CORRECTED 2026-09-18 after the kimi seat named it in the §F2 adjudication: &#x27;a host kill and a log flood are different classes&#x27; KEYS ON IMPACT, which is a CORRELATE of the defect -- committed in the very sentence excluding a row, during a session spent cataloguing that error. ***  The reopening rule&#x27;s test is its own parenthetical, SO ONE FIX DESIGN COVERS BOTH.  The filing stands and is stronger than the reason given: (1) NOT the same device -- sgi_ip30_2 is a separate DEVICE_ACCESS and registration, and sharing a .c file is not sharing a device; (2) NOT one fix design -- #531 de-fatalises, while this arm computes the right value and needs member 1 DELETE; (3) NOT the same detector -- no row reaches that offset, so it needs a NEW ORACLE, which the rule excludes outright.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — Found while checking #531&#x27;s four sites -- a FIFTH guest-reachable fatal() that the round&#x27;s scope correctly excludes. In its words: &#x27;Not one of the four sites. File it.&#x27; — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_215210/grok.txt; src/devices/dev_sgi_ip30.c</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ip30setisrassign</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_ip30.c set_isr (0x10020) ASSIGNS where the set/clear register pair implies OR: d-&gt;isr = idata clobbers every other pending interrupt bit, while the sibling clear_isr at 0x10028 is a correct AND-NOT</span><span class="tip">dev_sgi_ip30.c set_isr (0x10020) ASSIGNS where the set/clear register pair implies OR: d-&gt;isr = idata clobbers every other pending interrupt bit, while the sibling clear_isr at 0x10028 is a correct AND-NOT — note: FILED 2026-09-17 from the #531 pass 2.  *** FLAGGED BY grok AT DESIGN TIME AND CARRIED BY NO QUEUE ROW UNTIL NOW -- the unread-review class, which this project has paid for three times. ***  MEASURED by the opus5 seat on the shipped build: set 0x00ff then 0x5a00 gives ISR 0x5a00, NOT 0x5aff; while set 0x00ff then clear 0x000f correctly gives 0x00f0.  A set/clear register PAIR exists precisely because the set is an OR -- otherwise the clear register would be redundant -- so `d-&gt;isr = idata&#x27; loses every bit a concurrent source has raised.  #531 invokes heart.h&#x27;s authority for two decisions in these very arms (write-side at 0x10020/0x10028, read-only at 0x10030) and leaves this third one untouched.  NOT folded into #531 deliberately: it is a BEHAVIOUR change to a path #531 only de-fatalises, and the addition licence wants a measured delta or a transferred warrant rather than &#x27;while we are here&#x27;.  #531 records it as ASSESSED, NOT CHANGED.  WITNESS when taken: the #531 probe&#x27;s vehicle reaches this arm already -- seed via 0x10020, set a disjoint mask, read back at 0x10030.  One session.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — set 0x00ff then 0x5a00 -&gt; 0x5a00 (not 0x5aff); set 0x00ff then clear 0x000f -&gt; 0x00f0.  The clear is right and the set is not. — evidence: regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/opus_killtable_20260917/isrsem.log</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ip22legacyabort</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_ip22.c carries TWO more guest-reachable fatal()+abort() pairs in the write arms of local0/local1 irq mask -- the same message family as #531&#x27;s four, on an advertised machine</span><span class="tip">dev_sgi_ip22.c carries TWO more guest-reachable fatal()+abort() pairs in the write arms of local0/local1 irq mask -- the same message family as #531&#x27;s four, on an advertised machine — note: FILED 2026-09-17 by the #531 pass-2 opus5 seat as the nearest SIBLING of ip30abortkill.  Two structurally identical `fatal(&quot;TODO: ip22 legacy interrupt rewrite!&quot;); abort();&#x27; pairs in dev_sgi_ip22.c, in the WRITE arms of the local0 and local1 interrupt masks.  *** THE CENSUS LESSON RECURS: these carry no `exit(&#x27;, so the same three exit( sweeps that walked past #531&#x27;s four sites walk past these too.  A census keyed on `exit(&#x27; cannot see a host death spelled abort(). ***  Ranked HIGH on the same warrant as #531: guest-reachable host death on an advertised machine. The disposition is likely the same as #531&#x27;s -- heart.h has no ip22 equivalent, so the option-set work starts from the file&#x27;s own sibling handlers.  LINE NUMBERS VERIFIED 2026-09-17 by opening the file: :314 and :338 exactly, 2 abort() and 0 exit( in dev_sgi_ip22.c.  Grade upgraded BELIEVED -&gt; CONFIRMED.  *** SEVERITY CORRECTED HIGH -&gt; LOW THE SAME DAY IT WAS FILED, AND THE CORRECTION IS MINE TO OWN: I RANKED IT AS A SIBLING OF #531 WITHOUT CHECKING THE ONE FACT THAT MADE #531 LIVE. ***  #531&#x27;s warrant is that `-E sgi -e ip30&#x27; is an ADVERTISED machine AND the device is actually installed -- its rung-3 probe reached the code and killed the host, empirically.  dev_sgi_ip22 is DIFFERENT and the difference is decisive:  (a) `dev_sgi_ip22_init&#x27; has exactly TWO call sites in the whole tree, machine_sgi.c:270 and :278, and BOTH ARE COMMENTED OUT;  (b) the file defines DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_ip22) but NO DEVINIT(sgi_ip22), so the autodev / device_add route cannot register it either;  (c) it still COMPILES IN (Makefile.skel lists dev_sgi_ip22.o), which is why an abort()-census finds it and a reachability question does not get asked.  *** THE DEVICE IS NEVER IN ANY MACHINE&#x27;S MEMORY MAP, SO NO GUEST CAN REACH EITHER abort(). ***  CONSEQUENCE FOR THE WITNESS LADDER, and it is why this cannot simply be &#x27;done later&#x27;: NO WITNESS EXISTS OR CAN EXIST AT RUNGS 2-4 while the device is uninstalled, and installing it to reach the site is exactly what the ladder names as LAUNDERING -- &#x27;a machine or device_add introduced IN ORDER TO reach the site is laundering, not witnessing&#x27;.  So this is DOCUMENTED-ONLY unless and until something installs the device for its own reasons.  IT REMAINS WORTH KEEPING rather than dropping: the sites are dead code carrying a host kill, and the day anyone re-enables those two commented lines they become live immediately, with no review in between.  *** THE CENSUS LESSON STANDS UNCHANGED AND IS THE REAL VALUE HERE: 2 abort() and 0 exit( in this file, so the same three exit( sweeps that walked past #531&#x27;s four sites walk past these two. ***  A census keyed on `exit(&#x27; cannot see a host death spelled abort() -- whether or not the code is reachable.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — UNREACHABLE BY CONSTRUCTION -- the device is never installed.  Severity corrected HIGH -&gt; LOW.  The opus5 seat was right that it is a real sibling worth a row; the HIGH ranking was mine and was made without a reachability check, which is the one question #531&#x27;s own warrant turns on. — evidence: src/machines/machine_sgi.c:270,:278 (both commented); src/devices/dev_sgi_ip22.c:277 DEVICE_ACCESS with no DEVINIT; src/devices/Makefile.skel:26</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — Reported as :314-315 and :338-339.  An honest BELIEVED: a seat&#x27;s line numbers in this project have been wrong before when a different tree was read.  *** CONFIRMED by the main loop, and the line numbers were EXACT: :314 and :338, with 2 abort() and ZERO exit( in the whole file.  The census lesson holds literally -- the same three exit( sweeps that missed #531&#x27;s four sites miss these two for the identical reason. *** — evidence: src/devices/dev_sgi_ip22.c:314, :338</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">seatprescriptiondiff</span><span class="ttl">a row prescribed BY NAME in a seat&#x27;s fix-design answer can be lost even when #490&#x27;s diff-against-the-PRETABLE rule is obeyed, because the prescription never entered the PRETABLE -- and a PRETABLE that mis-classifies the cell CERTIFIES the omission</span><span class="tip">a row prescribed BY NAME in a seat&#x27;s fix-design answer can be lost even when #490&#x27;s diff-against-the-PRETABLE rule is obeyed, because the prescription never entered the PRETABLE -- and a PRETABLE that mis-classifies the cell CERTIFIES the omission — note: *** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST PRECISE: THE MISSING ROW WAS PRESCRIBED BY NAME, IN ONE LINE, IN A DURABLE ARTIFACT, BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED. ***  regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt:239 reads: &#x27;5. `isr 20 rd&#x27; / `isr 28 rd&#x27; -- load does not clobber ISR; load&#x27;s VALUE is not the seeded ISR (don&#x27;t-mirror)&#x27;.  BOTH rows, explicitly.  The round shipped `isr 20 rd val&#x27; and not its sibling, and it then cost a full extra kill table and a nine-seat adjudication pass to rediscover -- with agy REQUIRING it and grok having to point at its own earlier answer.  The opus5 seat independently reported the same pattern: grok&#x27;s design pass prescribed six closing rows and items 1, 2, 4 and 5 did not ship.  *** THE STRUCTURAL GAP, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: THE CARRIER&#x27;S EXISTING RULE CANNOT CATCH THIS. ***  After #490 the rule is &#x27;a row list rewritten from a panel&#x27;s findings is DIFFED against the pre-table block&#x27;s own prescribed rows, not merely extended with the new ones.&#x27;  That binds the PRETABLE.  Here the prescription NEVER ENTERED A PRETABLE -- it lived in a SEAT&#x27;S FIX-DESIGN ANSWER, and nothing diffs a row list against that.  So the #490 rule was obeyed and the row was still lost: the diff ran against the wrong source document.  AND THE PRETABLE MADE IT WORSE RATHER THAN NEUTRAL: PRETABLE.out listed BOTH read arms as &#x27;decline, no value to pin&#x27;, so the one artifact #490 DOES mandate diffing against positively asserted that neither row was owed.  *A pre-table block that mis-classifies a cell does not merely fail to catch the omission; it CERTIFIES it.*  grok&#x27;s correction is one sentence: A DECLINE HAS A VALUE, AND IT IS 0.  PROPOSED REMEDY, FILED NOT BUILT (a harness change owing two negative controls): a round whose fix design was reviewed by a seat DIFFS ITS FINAL ROW LIST AGAINST THAT SEAT&#x27;S PRESCRIBED ROWS, as #490 mandates for the PRETABLE -- and the PRETABLE should CARRY those prescriptions forward so the two documents cannot disagree silently.  Cheapest mechanical form is existence-only: if a FIXDIFF_&lt;seat&gt;_*.txt sits beside the killtable dir, each of its numbered prescriptions must map to a shipped row or an explicit no-row sentence.  *** THE LAST FIVE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260827_134019).  23 OF 23 NOW READ -- the section-G backlog is CLEARED. ***  This was a pass-1 DESIGN panel on a now-CLOSED row, so it was read for the question `seatprescriptiondiff&#x27; exists to ask: DID THE PRESCRIPTIONS SURVIVE INTO THE SHIPPED WORK?  *** AND THE ANSWER IS YES -- A NEGATIVE RESULT THAT BOUNDS THE ROW I FILED THIS MORNING. ***  agy prescribed three items.  (1) the predicate fix -- SHIPPED, dev_wdc.c now triggers on WDCTL_RST (0x04) and not WDCTL_4BIT (0x08), with the rising-edge `prev_ctl&#x27; machinery and a recorded brace hazard beside it.  (2) the MACHINE_LANDISK alt-status mapping -- NOT in dev_wdc.c, and NOT a silent drop: it was SPLIT INTO ITS OWN ROW `landiskaux&#x27;, which is open, and OUTSTANDING_BUGS carries the coupling in full.  (3) acceptance rows -- folded, per the kimi/deepseek rulings in the same panel.  *** SO `seatprescriptiondiff&#x27; HAS A POPULATION OF ONE (the #531 case), NOT A PATTERN.  A class filed from a single instance is a hypothesis, and this is the first test of it -- it did not generalise.  Recorded as such rather than left looking systemic. ***  AND THE COUPLING WAS HANDLED IN THE SAFE ORDER, which is worth keeping because the unsafe one was live: mapping the landisk register WHILE the predicate still triggered on 0x08 would have latched COMMAND_RESET on SIX routine Device Control writes per probe (a seat measured twelve writes, six of them 0x08, currently all landing on unmapped memory).  The round fixed the PREDICATE first, so `wdcaltctrlbit&#x27; is CLOSED as overtaken and `landiskaux&#x27; is now OPEN AND UNBLOCKED.  *** THAT MAKES `landiskaux&#x27; A ONE-LINE FIX THAT IS NOW SAFE TO TAKE -- a strong critical-and-easily-resolvable candidate for the next round. ***  The other seats&#x27; content was design judgement, no live defects: glm listed SEVEN uninlined premises and ranked them by load-bearingness (the premise-audit discipline working as intended); minimax named an option-set hole (C4/B5, &#x27;the brief&#x27;s C-options jump&#x27;); deepseek ruled that a channel reset must clear in-flight transfer, FIFO, phase and interrupt state but NOT per-device-life diagnostics like `warned_*&#x27; -- and dev_wdc.c carries 43 `warned_&#x27; references, so that distinction is load-bearing there.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — Measured instance: #531 shipped `isr 20 rd val&#x27; and not `isr 28 rd val&#x27;, both of which that one line prescribes.  Cost: one extra kill table, one nine-seat pass. — evidence: regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt:239</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** THE LAST FIVE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260827_134019).  23 OF 23 NOW READ -- the section-G backlog is CLEARED. ***  This was a pass-1 DESIGN panel on a now-CLOSED row, so it was read for the question `seatprescriptiondiff&#x27; exists to ask: DID THE PRESCRIPTIONS SURVIVE INTO THE SHIPPED WORK?  *** AND THE ANSWER IS YES -- A NEGATIVE RESULT THAT BOUNDS THE ROW I FILED THIS MORNING. ***  agy prescribed three items.  (1) the predicate fix -- SHIPPED, dev_wdc.c now triggers on WDCTL_RST (0x04) and not WDCTL_4BIT (0x08), with the rising-edge `prev_ctl&#x27; machinery and a recorded brace hazard beside it.  (2) the MACHINE_LANDISK alt-status mapping -- NOT in dev_wdc.c, and NOT a silent drop: it was SPLIT INTO ITS OWN ROW `landiskaux&#x27;, which is open, and OUTSTANDING_BUGS carries the coupling in full.  (3) acceptance rows -- folded, per the kimi/deepseek rulings in the same panel.  *** SO `seatprescriptiondiff&#x27; HAS A POPULATION OF ONE (the #531 case), NOT A PATTERN.  A class filed from a single instance is a hypothesis, and this is the first test of it -- it did not generalise.  Recorded as such rather than left looking systemic. ***  AND THE COUPLING WAS HANDLED IN THE SAFE ORDER, which is worth keeping because the unsafe one was live: mapping the landisk register WHILE the predicate still triggered on 0x08 would have latched COMMAND_RESET on SIX routine Device Control writes per probe (a seat measured twelve writes, six of them 0x08, currently all landing on unmapped memory).  The round fixed the PREDICATE first, so `wdcaltctrlbit&#x27; is CLOSED as overtaken and `landiskaux&#x27; is now OPEN AND UNBLOCKED.  *** THAT MAKES `landiskaux&#x27; A ONE-LINE FIX THAT IS NOW SAFE TO TAKE -- a strong critical-and-easily-resolvable candidate for the next round. ***  The other seats&#x27; content was design judgement, no live defects: glm listed SEVEN uninlined premises and ranked them by load-bearingness (the premise-audit discipline working as intended); minimax named an option-set hole (C4/B5, &#x27;the brief&#x27;s C-options jump&#x27;); deepseek ruled that a channel reset must clear in-flight transfer, FIFO, phase and interrupt state but NOT per-device-life diagnostics like `warned_*&#x27; -- and dev_wdc.c carries 43 `warned_&#x27; references, so that distinction is load-bearing there. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_134019/*.txt; src/devices/dev_wdc.c; OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:12574-12600</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">seatsreadburnsplit</span><span class="ttl">check_seats_read.py counts THINKING BURNS and genuinely unread answers as one number -- measured 2 burns among 25, whose disposition (SEAT DOWN) is the opposite of an unread review&#x27;s (read it)</span><span class="tip">check_seats_read.py counts THINKING BURNS and genuinely unread answers as one number -- measured 2 burns among 25, whose disposition (SEAT DOWN) is the opposite of an unread review&#x27;s (read it) — note: *** THE GATE&#x27;S &#x27;25 SEAT ANSWERS RECORDED NOWHERE&#x27; CONFLATES TWO OPPOSITE THINGS, AND THE SPLIT IS 2 / 23.  Measured 2026-09-18 by testing every one of the 25 for an empty `response&#x27; field, which is the burn discriminator this project already uses. ***  TWO ARE THINKING BURNS -- SEAT FAILURES, NOT UNREAD REVIEWS: panel_20260826_222557/ollama_deepseek (response 0 chars, thinking 142,280, done_reason=length, eval_count=65536 -- THE burn the carrier documents by its byte count) and panel_20260907_104814/ollama_glm (response 0, thinking 114,797, done_reason=**stop**, eval_count=None -- the variant where BOTH signature fields read NORMAL and only the empty response is diagnostic).  There is nothing to read in either; the honest record is SEAT DOWN.  TWENTY-THREE ARE REAL ANSWERS AND ARE GENUINELY UNREAD, totalling roughly 400 KB.  The largest is panel_20260907_184039/ollama_glm at 243,346 chars -- and it is a burn that panel_ollama.py RECOVERED by re-firing with think=False, so the tooling saved a 121 KB answer that nobody then opened.  *** THE CHECKER GAP: check_seats_read.py counts a BURN and an UNREAD ANSWER identically, and their dispositions are opposite -- one is a seat failure to record, the other is content to read that may hold a defect.  That is this project&#x27;s own &#x27;a blank cell and a seat failure must not look alike&#x27; rule, and the checker enforcing it is itself blind to the distinction. ***  FILED as `seatsreadburnsplit&#x27;.  TRIAGED, NOT READ -- stated plainly so the record does not overclaim: this cycle established WHICH of the 25 are readable and which are dead, and read none of the 23.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** THE GATE&#x27;S &#x27;25 SEAT ANSWERS RECORDED NOWHERE&#x27; CONFLATES TWO OPPOSITE THINGS, AND THE SPLIT IS 2 / 23.  Measured 2026-09-18 by testing every one of the 25 for an empty `response&#x27; field, which is the burn discriminator this project already uses. ***  TWO ARE THINKING BURNS -- SEAT FAILURES, NOT UNREAD REVIEWS: panel_20260826_222557/ollama_deepseek (response 0 chars, thinking 142,280, done_reason=length, eval_count=65536 -- THE burn the carrier documents by its byte count) and panel_20260907_104814/ollama_glm (response 0, thinking 114,797, done_reason=**stop**, eval_count=None -- the variant where BOTH signature fields read NORMAL and only the empty response is diagnostic).  There is nothing to read in either; the honest record is SEAT DOWN.  TWENTY-THREE ARE REAL ANSWERS AND ARE GENUINELY UNREAD, totalling roughly 400 KB.  The largest is panel_20260907_184039/ollama_glm at 243,346 chars -- and it is a burn that panel_ollama.py RECOVERED by re-firing with think=False, so the tooling saved a 121 KB answer that nobody then opened.  *** THE CHECKER GAP: check_seats_read.py counts a BURN and an UNREAD ANSWER identically, and their dispositions are opposite -- one is a seat failure to record, the other is content to read that may hold a defect.  That is this project&#x27;s own &#x27;a blank cell and a seat failure must not look alike&#x27; rule, and the checker enforcing it is itself blind to the distinction. ***  FILED as `seatsreadburnsplit&#x27;.  TRIAGED, NOT READ -- stated plainly so the record does not overclaim: this cycle established WHICH of the 25 are readable and which are dead, and read none of the 23. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260826_222557/ollama_deepseek.txt; _scratchpad/panel_20260907_104814/ollama_glm.txt; the other 23 by the same test</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">devwritedata</span><span class="ttl">memory_rw hands devices a non-const data[] and store_buf casts const away: a handler that assigns data[...] on a WRITE corrupts its caller&#x27;s source buffer (for PROM getenv, a host string or .rodata)</span><span class="tip">memory_rw hands devices a non-const data[] and store_buf casts const away: a handler that assigns data[...] on a WRITE corrupts its caller&#x27;s source buffer (for PROM getenv, a host string or .rodata) — witness: none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c tok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">∅</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — [NOT A SEAT ANSWER] (#534 R2: was the stage-scoped no-panel token, which this entry never meant)  Filed from fable5&#x27;s lcalenzerodrop Q2 ruling: the host-heap overwrite Opus measured is mutant-only, but it exposes the class.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lcawidefold</span><span class="ttl">#530 lca_isa forwards ONE byte of an N-byte store_buf store, and further 16-byte chunks fold onto the same port via &gt;&gt;5 -- a policy over an input the 21066 cannot express; documented-only</span><span class="tip">#530 lca_isa forwards ONE byte of an N-byte store_buf store, and further 16-byte chunks fold onto the same port via &gt;&gt;5 -- a policy over an input the 21066 cannot express; documented-only — witness: none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c tok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">∅</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — [NOT A SEAT ANSWER] (#534 R2: was the stage-scoped no-panel token, which this entry never meant)  CLOSE6_VERDICT&#x27;s `decide separately&#x27; residual, discharged as documented-only by the fable5 adjudication of 2026-09-18. || 2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  *** THE REOPEN TRIGGER HAS FIRED. ***  This row&#x27;s recorded condition was *a guest found issuing PROM GETENV into the LCA ISA window*.  `regress/lca_widewrite_probe.py` IS that guest, and it measures the behaviour this row describes: a 16-byte store_buf write reaches the port as ONE byte, s[(P &amp; 3) % len], and the other fifteen are dropped.  **Nothing here changes that behaviour** -- the width row pins that the store is not DECLINED, which is #530&#x27;s floor; whether forwarding one byte of N is acceptable is this row&#x27;s question, and it is now live rather than hypothetical. || 2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  Annotated from #530&#x27;s pass 2 (kimi-k3:cloud, packet-fed, derived from the inlined store_buf): a GETENV whose a2 is MISALIGNED fails every alignment test down to psize 1 and is issued as sixteen 1-byte writes, each taking the write arm with ofs 0 -- so all sixteen bytes ARE forwarded, in order.  The row&#x27;s &#x27;15 of 16 dropped&#x27; is therefore alignment-dependent, and its contract should say so.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcrstbitonly</span><span class="ttl">dev_wdc.c aux handler&#x27;s `if (idata &amp; WDCTL_RST)&#x27; is pinned by NO wired row: `if (idata)&#x27; (reset on ANY nonzero Device Control byte) escapes the whole battery</span><span class="tip">dev_wdc.c aux handler&#x27;s `if (idata &amp; WDCTL_RST)&#x27; is pinned by NO wired row: `if (idata)&#x27; (reset on ANY nonzero Device Control byte) escapes the whole battery — witness: none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c tok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">∅</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — [NOT A SEAT ANSWER] (#534 R2: was the stage-scoped no-panel token, which this entry never meant)  Found by glm on #533 pass 2; the carrier I first named for it was measured false by fable5.  wdc_altoffset_probe.py P3 writes 0x02 at OFFSET 1 (declined before :832); wdc_altdigin_probe.py writes only 0x06 at offset 0; wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py is unwired and writes 0x0e.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">fiqenableread</span><span class="ttl">dev_footbridge.c: a READ of FIQ_ENABLE_SET (+0x288, DC21285 FIQEnable per Table 7-10) returns 0 instead of d-&gt;fiq_enable -- the IRQ sibling at +0x188 computes the value (then exits)</span><span class="tip">dev_footbridge.c: a READ of FIQ_ENABLE_SET (+0x288, DC21285 FIQEnable per Table 7-10) returns 0 instead of d-&gt;fiq_enable -- the IRQ sibling at +0x188 computes the value (then exits) — witness: none — note: [2026-10-02] RE-GRADED LOW -&gt; MED by #554 from the Astra review campaign (retro, DISPOSITIONS.md:791, for the readback defect; the files are under regress/artifacts/astraretro_20260926/ and astraretro_harness_20260926/).  </span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c tok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">∅</span><span class="tip">2026-09-19 — [NOT A SEAT ANSWER] Scope from #539&#x27;s pass-1 adjudication (fable5 D3): FIQ is unmodelled end-to-end, ONE row, FIVE facets derived from the code -- (1) +0x288 read returns 0, not d-&gt;fiq_enable; (2) +0x28c read returns 0 silently while +0x18c ends the process; (3) fiq_status is never written while the datasheet requires identical raw status (dc21285.txt:6886); (4) no FIQ output in the struct; (5) FIQSoft +0x290 has no case.  First witness = codex p1&#x27;s write-P-then-read +0x288 row (today 0, hardware P).</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><sup class="b b-s">s</sup><span class="tip">2026-09-19 — a review by the same model family — [DISSENT, recorded verbatim under the late-dissent rule, disposition 3] Opus 5 seat, fbdeclinefive pass 1: &quot;28Ch (FIQEnableClear) -- the exact FIQ twin of 18Ch -- ALREADY declines silently and returns 0 today ... the cheapest resolution is to give 28Ch the same latched announce in the same commit (one more arm, one more bit; it is the same class and the same file).&quot;  Ruled NOT #545&#x27;s round by fable5 on the reopening rule&#x27;s WITNESS clause (a +0x28c read returns 0 before and after; a host log line is a detector channel, not a symptom) and because one field takes the controller from four dispositions to three -- the FIQ half is this ONE row.  #545&#x27;s probe header states the asymmetry.  Facet (6) candidate IRQSoft filed as its own row fbirqsoftunimpl. — evidence: regress/artifacts/fbd5/p1_20260919/opus_p1/OPUS_P1_ANSWER.md</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c tok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">∅</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — [NOT A SEAT ANSWER] Filed from grok&#x27;s n6 assess answer (sibling-parity, outside the kill census).</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c non