t MSR (MSR CPSR_c,#0x14 = 0xE321F014) ends the emulator PROCESS at arm_load_register_bank()&#x27;s default: -- a reserved M[4:0] is UNPREDICTABLE, not fatal — commits: 4f908ad5 — #562 CLOSES.  msr_imm, arm_pop&#x27;s ^ restore and the DPI S-to-PC restore decline the MODE FIELD of a value naming a mode this model does not bank (the rest applies), announced once per site per CPU; Save/Load&#x27;s default: arms are latched backstops (no exit(1)).  An emulator POLICY for UNPREDICTABLE (DDI 0100I A2.5.7), not architecture; codex&#x27;s whole-operation dissent recorded.  The detector (arm_msrmode_probe.py, 47 rows, 43 sessions, a generated grid) took five repairs; repair 5&#x27;s tables: 110 runs, 104 distinct mutants, 103 red, M_msr_argmask disclosed.  The final pass&#x27;s latch-clear residue and two measured closures are filed (armmsrmodedebt) by owner ruling 2026-10-09.  Gate 14: run 1 caught a STALE dev_i80321.o (#562 grew struct arm_cpu; the build tracks no header dependencies) -- every build tree rebuilt clean, run 2 PASS, 1110 of 1110.  Filed: armusrstop, armspsrthumb, armmmuvehicle, armmsrmodedebt, headerstaleobj.  GPT-6 Astra (ultra) confirmed with changes (13), all records corrections, all applied.  Fable 5.1 was deliberately not fired on any pass (owner, 2026-10-07); badge A: the main loop (Opus 5.5) adjudicated each pass.  After the owner lifted the hold, Fable 5.1 adjudicated the main loop&#x27;s nine rulings before the commit, 2026-10-09 (regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/fable_adjudication_20261009/fable5_adjudication562.md): RATIFY WITH CHANGES (3), all nine upheld, the three records corrections applied. — witness: probe:regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py -- rung 3 on HEAD ef577140&#x27;s unmodified -E testarm: the 17-row witness 14/17 (regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/witness_20261007/run_head.out: kill 14, kill 15, save 2s dead, every control green); the shipped 47-row detector 18/47 on HEAD with 29 named reds, and 47/47 on the fix (regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/killtable5_20261008/run_head.out, run_fixed.out) — note: [2026-10-09] #562 SHIPPED -- the commits field is owed to the next commit, with the pass-2 receipt.  CORRECTION (GPT-6 Astra item 4, Fable 5.1 R9): the IN WORK note below calls M_msr_argmask &#x27;the cached-translation write, which NO stepping row can see&#x27; -- FALSE: none of the SHIPPED 47 rows sees it, but the filed loop prototype does (regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/opus_pass2r5_20261009/probe48.py, KTR5_LANE5.out 47/48); the old text is kept below.  || #562 IN WORK 2026-10-07: rung-3 witness regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py HEAD 16/26 -&gt; fixed 26/26; 20-mutant table 0 escapes (regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/killtable_20261007/).  Pass 2 found two floor families (latch never revisited; SPSR source labels SVC-only) -&gt; repair + 36-mutant table running; the repair owes its own pass 2.  Fable 5.1 deliberately not fired (owner, 2026-10-07).  Opus 5.5 pass 2 (artifacts/armmsrmodekill/opus_pass2_20261007/, measured): SHIP-WITH-CHANGES, 3 floor breaches -- F1 latch-line deletions 26/26 at MSR/POP/DPI/LOAD; F2 NEW `mask &amp;= ~0x0f` 26/26 (all driven MSR values have bit 4 set; `kill 03` row owed); F3 restore-mode axis SVC-only.  All three sent to the repair.  REPAIR (fix2, probe 26-&gt;38 rows) applied 2026-10-07 ~23:00: tables 2+3 all 36+6 new mutants red, 0 escapes.  Repair&#x27;s own pass 2 (panel_20261007_230103, copied to artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r_20261007): codex DO-NOT-SHIP (5 static breaches: return_flag / DPI PC-dest / DPI S enclosing predicates; label x latch at both restores), grok DO-NOT-SHIP (restore mode-keep mask bits 0/1/3 unpinned -- same CLASS as Opus F2; pop x zero-SPSR; Load backstop comment still false; I/F contract overclaim), deepseek DO-NOT-SHIP (spsr_copied gate), glm + kimi-k3:cloud SHIP-WITH-CHANGES, agy + minimax SHIP; kimi CLI 403 (subscription) -&gt; covered by kimi-k3:cloud.  All predictions sent to the Opus 5.5 seat to MEASURE.  Same class twice (mask width; latch x label) -&gt; next repair owes CLASS CLOSURE.  Opus 5.5 MEASURED the repair (artifacts/armmsrmodekill/opus_pass2r_20261007/): 21 of 23 mutants 38/38 -- helper ARM_FLAG_MODE-&gt;0xff (one token; declines msr cpsr_c,#0xd2), restore mask width x7, label x latch x outcome x6, I forced on decline x2, enclosing predicates x3 (return_flag, DPI rd==PC, non-S restore); killed: msr_imm ~0x1e (34/38), spsr_copied deleted (37/38).  Panel predictions CONFIRMED (grok, codex); agy + minimax SHIP votes refuted by measurement.  #499 pattern -&gt; REPAIR 3 = CLASS CLOSURE (A values/bits with I/F set, SPSR 0xcf/0xdf; B 40-cell label x latch x outcome; C enclosing-predicate session; D records) dispatched 2026-10-08.  REPAIR 3 (class closure) APPLIED 2026-10-08 ~06:3x: probe 40 rows; fixed 40/40, HEAD 17/40; 65 mutants (Opus&#x27;s 23 + earlier 42) ALL red (artifacts killtable3_20261008/); product code = comments only vs repair 2; build trees 24/24 two-part clear.  Its pass 2 FIRED (brief PASS2R3_brief_20261008.md, shared findings + 9 dispositions) + Opus seat measuring.  Repair-3 pass 2 (artifacts panel_pass2r3_20261008): codex DO-NOT-SHIP 2 + grok DO-NOT-SHIP 1 (static) -- helper projection widened to undriven bits (0x3f, |C; driven upper union 0xc00000c0) and decline flag provenance (old CPSR N/Z already equals SPSR N/Z at every decline); glm/deepseek/kimi-k3 SHIP-WITH-CHANGES; agy/minimax SHIP; kimi CLI 403.  Backstop comments now ratified by codex + grok.  Sent to the Opus seat to MEASURE.  Opus 5.5 MEASURED repair 3 (artifacts opus_pass2r3_20261008/): 14/20 fix-side mutants 40/40 -- helper projection width (0x3f, |C), decline flag provenance x7, S-instance enclosing cells (DPI rd==PC under S; pop return_flag||s_bit); its 42-row closure probe kills all 14.  Owner ruling 2026-10-08: close, one more review pass, commit.  REPAIR 4 dispatched (adopt Opus probe, closure rule: every operand bit set in an accept per path; every restored bit differs between sources at each decline; full 85-mutant re-table).  REPAIR 4 DONE 2026-10-08 ~15:4x (artifacts killtable4_20261008/): product code unchanged (fix4 == fix3); probe 43 rows (Opus closure probe + `msr wide`); fixed 43/43, HEAD 17/43; 91 mutants ALL red, none equivalent; closure rule checked per path.  FINAL pass 2 FIRED (PASS2R4_brief_20261008.md) + Opus measuring.  FINAL pass on repair 4 (artifacts panel_pass2r4_20261008): codex DO-NOT-SHIP 3 + grok DO-NOT-SHIP 1 (static) -- helper NARROWED by bit 3 (`&amp; 0x17` admits 0x18-0x1a; no driven value crosses that boundary), decline FORCING a constant (e.g. `| ARM_FLAG_N`; the old/new-differ rule does not catch forcing the expected polarity), msr decline bit narrowed at one label (20/30 label x bit cells), pop enclosing cell non-S/no-PC; records: &#x27;at EVERY decline&#x27; false for T, 0xffffffd1 bits 3:1 clear, inline c00000df comments stale.  glm/deepseek/minimax/kimi-k3 SHIP-WITH-CHANGES, agy SHIP, kimi CLI 403.  Opus measuring the same candidates.  Opus MEASURED on repair 4 (partial, 16:14): 4/4 escape 43/43 -- H_narrow_17, M_msr_clr0f_iaus, N_pop_Tlatch, N_dpi_forceN.  Owner ruling 2026-10-08: REPAIR 5 = GENERATED full grid (bit x polarity x path x outcome; msr label x bit; helper boundary values; pop 4 enclosing cells), one review pass, commit.  Dispatched.  Opus final measurement on repair 4 (artifacts opus_pass2r4_20261008/): 18 mutants, 15 escape 43/43 in 4 classes (A helper narrowing H_narrow_17 + H_case_18; B msr decline width per label x4; C restore forced/OR-ed bits x4; D T while latched x4) + M_msr_argmask; 3 controls killed.  All fed to repair 5&#x27;s second table.  REPAIR 5 (generated grid) MEASURED 2026-10-09 (artifacts killtable5_20261008/): 47 rows, 43 sessions; fixed 47/47, HEAD 18/47 (29 reds); 110 runs / 104 distinct mutants (the earlier set + Opus&#x27;s 18), 103 red, ONE escape: M_msr_argmask 47/47 -- the cached-translation write, which NO stepping row can see: debugger() clears every dyntrans translation when it interacts, so each re-execution is re-translated.  Repair 5&#x27;s NOT CLAIMED said the grid pinned the register form -- FALSE; corrected comment-only (AST-identical to the measured probe) and installed.  The helper agent hit the Opus weekly limit after the tables finished; results collected from its clone.  Final pass next.  FINAL pass on repair 5 (artifacts panel_pass2r5_20261009): grok SHIP-WITH-CHANGES 0 floor breaches (records: G1&#x27;s lead sentence false; PRETABLE5 sec.2 superseded; gate MEASURED placeholder); codex DO-NOT-SHIP 1 class, STATIC: a latch-CLEAR decline forcing a bit 31:8 at DPI/pop unobserved (48 of 72 cells), against the contract&#x27;s &#x27;forcing ... with the latch clear or set&#x27;; deepseek DO-NOT-SHIP (M_msr_argmask loop row owed); glm/agy/kimi-k3 SHIP-WITH-CHANGES; minimax SHIP; kimi CLI 403.  M_msr_argmask: grok + codex grade DISCLOSURE under the bounded DEPENDS-ON rule, deepseek owed.  Codex&#x27;s prediction sent to the Opus seat to MEASURE; owner to be asked before any repair 6.  || filed 2026-09-17 from a VOLUNTEERED grok side-finding (raised under the standing license while it was refuting a different brief), then CONFIRMED on a second firing by a full instruction-to-switch trace.  ONE ROUND, MEMBER 3 at BOTH default: arms.  *** SAVE (:559) IS NOT FIRST-HIT -- it runs on the OLD mode (SVC32, modelled) and returns; the process dies in LOAD (:606).  A LOAD-ONLY fix CREATES the Save kill, which is sibling parity and not a second row. ***  Member 1 DELETE is WRONG (leaves cpsr reserved and r[] on the previous bank -&gt; silent wrong-bank execution, then a second kill).  Member 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE on ARM but wrong: MSR is &#x27;Exceptions: None&#x27;.  THE DECLINE MUST SIT BEFORE SAVE, or roll cpsr back in Load -- callers write cpsr BETWEEN save and load.  RANKED ABOVE armthumbabortkill: process death vs running=0, cheaper witness, no fake-green hazard.  GATE 14&#x27;s 358 checks never feed the encoding.  *** REWRITE, NOT PATCH.  THE 16-ROW SPEC PLUS EIGHT AMENDMENTS DOES NOT CLOSE, AND THE SEAT THAT WROTE THE ORIGINAL SPEC RULED AGAINST ITS OWN WORK WHEN ASKED TO BE HARDER ON IT. ***  I asked plainly whether eight amendments on a 16-row spec meant the spec should be rewritten rather than patched, and named the `#392` shape as the thing I feared.  Answer: yes.  &#x27;A builder applying them in order will get 1+6 wrong and will either treat `save 2s` as a duplicate of `kill 15` or REPLACE `kill 15` with it, losing the `== 0x14` pin.&#x27;  *** AND THE TALLY ITSELF WAS A FROZEN NUMBER: it is 14/17 -&gt; 17/17 over THIRTEEN sessions, not 14/16 -&gt; 16/16 over twelve.  The original 12 was already false -- 16 scored minus `enc ok` minus the identity row minus one for live/pc/armed sharing S1 is 13, and the spec wrote 12. ***  THE VEHICLE STANDS and is unchanged: `-V -A -E testarm -M 64`, `0xE321F014`, member 3 at BOTH `default:` arms, one fresh process per scored row that executes a guest instruction, mid-stream dump.  The ROW LIST, the ORACLES and the SESSION ARITHMETIC are replaced. **ANSWER TO MY INTERACTION QUESTION 1+6, AND IT REFUTES MY FRAMING.**  I asked whether, once amendment 1 makes EOF-alone the kill trigger, the `enc ok` / `no cbit` / dump-before-MSR machinery becomes THE ONLY THING between the witness and a false green.  The answer is that those three are **NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT** -- each has a smallest defeat: dump-DEST proves MRS ran, not that the MSR word was planted or stepped; `no cbit` is greened by a decoder that never writes the mode field AT ALL, which greens every kill row too; and `enc ok` walking a constructor instead of the planted literal is the BFX incident repeating.  What actually closes false-green, all four required: (1) **POST want is `m=13 sp once`, not `m=13`** -- member 3 prints `(once)`, and encoding-nop, skipped-step and a silent member-2 all print nothing, so the latch must be IN the got string or its absence is a comment rather than a FAIL; (2) **poison `DEST+4` with 0xdeadbeef** and dump DEST+4, never DEST+0, so a never-fired post-MSR store reds; (3) **one `WORDS` dict**, with every `put w` drawn from it and a per-session plant-dump of the MSR slot -- this is the load-bearing anti-false-green that amendment 6 hoped `enc ok` was; (4) **named-red tally** -- HEAD must be EXACTLY the three named reds, because 17/17 on HEAD is not success. **ANSWER TO INTERACTION 3+5, AND AMENDMENT 3 IS HALF-RIGHT AND MISREADS ITS OWN CASE.**  The original two reds were `kill 14` and `kill 15` -- TWO LOAD HOLES catching the `== 0x14` special case -- **not** Load vs Save, which is what amendment 3 assumed.  So: SCORE `save 2s`, KEEP `kill 15`, do NOT replace one with the other.  The underlying worry stands: without a scored two-write row a Load-only skip-memcpy POSTs clean and still dies on the write OUT of reserved mode (16/17, `save 2s` red on the second MSR -- that is the point of the row). **AMENDMENT 5 IS TRUE OF THE ENCODING AND INERT FOR THIS PROBE, VERIFIED LOCALLY BY ME AND NOT TAKEN FROM THE SEAT:** `#0x14` does clear I and F, and testarm does hang cons/ether/rtc off `irqc` -- but ARM IRQ dispatch sits inside `if (!single_step) {` at `cpu_dyntrans.c:206` with the ARM arm at `:208-209`, and `-V` sets single_step; **`fiq_asserted` has ZERO occurrences across all of `src/`** and `ARM_FLAG_F` zero in `cpu_dyntrans.c`, so there is no ARM FIQ dispatch to enable; and debugger interaction `timer_stop()`s so `step N` runs no host-timer callback.  **The pin is STAY ON `step`, NEVER `continue`** -- a `continue` after a LANDED `#0x14` can take `arm_exception` and run Save on the reserved current mode, which is a free-run hazard and not a step hazard.  DO NOT change the filed stimulus to `#0xD4`. **BOTH KILL ARMS CONFIRMED AT HEAD BY READING:** `cpu_arm.c:559-561` `arm_save_register_bank: unimplemented mode %i` + `exit(1)`, and `:606-608` the same for `arm_load_register_bank`.  Two arms, two kills -- which is exactly why member 3 is owed at both and why `save 2s` is independent rather than a duplicate. **DROPPED FROM THE ORIGINAL SPEC:** EOF-AND-text as the kill trigger; the 12 sessions; &#x27;two reds because two default: arms&#x27;; POST `0xd3` on `kill 14` (member 3 refuses the MODE FIELD, so I|F stay clear at `0x13` -- record I|F, do not want them); already-USR32 as the fake-green mechanism; copy-flags-`session()`; copy-flags-`printed()` (arm_flags_probe.py has none).  Amendment 8 belongs on the ALPHA spec, not this one. **CLAUSE (e), DISCLOSED NOT ROWED:** register-form MSR (`X(msr)` copies rm into arg[0] and calls msr_imm); the 26-bit holes; the other 32-bit holes 0x16/18/19/1a/1c/1d/1e (`kill 14` + `kill 15` catch a ONE-VALUE special case, not a two-value one); SPSR-then-`MOVS pc`; free-run IRQ after a landed reserved write, pinned by stay-on-step. **BUILD FROM THE ARTIFACT, NOT FROM THIS NOTE** -- the complete 17-row table, the 13-session schedule, the CONTROL=DEAD set, the plant layouts and the tally live verbatim at `regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/WITNESSSPEC_REWRITE_grok_20260917.txt`.  Transcribing a row table into a ledger note is the frozen-number class waiting to happen. **ONE BUILD REQUIREMENT I FOUND MYSELF AND THE SPEC DOES NOT STATE:** the launch line uses `regress/arm_bfx_stub.bin`, which is on the NEVER-STAGE list and is UNTRACKED.  That is safe, and the reason matters -- **all 11 top-level committed probes referencing such a stub CREATE IT IF ABSENT** (e.g. `alpha_harness_probe.py:169-173`).  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` MUST carry the same self-create block, or it will be the one committed probe that cannot run on a fresh clone.  *** THE PROBE WAS ATTACKED BEFORE IT WAS EVER RUN, AND IT HAD THREE INDEPENDENT DEFECTS. Verdict: &#x27;It runs.  It does not score.&#x27; ***  (1) **LINE LABELS ARE 16-BYTE ALIGNED** -- `debugger_cmds.c:709` is `addr = addr_start &amp; ~0xf`, so `dump 0x9004` labels the line `0x00009000` AND BLANK-PADS the leading word groups, shifting every group index.  (2) **THE DUMP RENDERS LITTLE-ENDIAN BYTES, NOT A WORD** -- 0x2a prints as `2a000000`.  (3) **`save 2s` READ A SLOT NOTHING WROTE**: its plant put SP at DEST+16 via `str_sp_10` while `classify()` read DEST+8, so ` sp` could NEVER appear and **the row was ALWAYS RED, pre-fix and post-fix alike** -- not a witness of Save at all. *** THE REMEDIES FOR (1) AND (2) ALREADY EXISTED IN THIS TREE AND I DID NOT USE THEM: `arm_bdt_probe.py:123-149` records the alignment measurement from 2026-09-11 in a seven-line comment, and `arm_flags_probe.py:191-193` carries `sw()` for the byte order. Knowledge present, unconsulted at the moment of use -- the carrier&#x27;s own recurring finding.  The repair COPIES both rather than inventing. *** **AND THE FAILURE MODE WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A FALSE GREEN, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  `live` wants 0x2a and would have got 0x2a000000; `armed` would have given `?/?`.  Both sit in the CONTROL=DEAD set, so the probe would have printed `ARMMSRMODE_CONTROL=DEAD`, `RESULT=0/0` and &#x27;SETUP FAILED -- NOT no defect&#x27;.  **The vehicle-kill design worked exactly as intended: it would have REFUSED TO SCORE rather than lying.**  That is the difference between a wasted run and a wrong record, and it is why the CONTROL=DEAD set is not decoration. *A self-check while repairing it showed why `arm_flags_probe.py` warns as it does: `deadbeef` read RAW yields 0xdeadbeef, so the POISON comparison would have MATCHED under the broken parser while every real value was wrong -- the sentinel&#x27;s own byte pattern hiding the defect.* **ONE CORRECTION RUNNING THE OTHER WAY, against the SPEC rather than the probe:** `print pc` wanting CODE+8 after `step 2` is RIGHT; the spec table&#x27;s CODE+4 is the wrong half, and a builder must not &#x27;fix&#x27; the probe down to it.  REPAIRED, AST clean, zero raw `dump` commands left.  **STILL NOT RUN** -- the adjudicated ship order puts the commit first, then this.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).** — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/REACH_grok_20260917.txt; src/cpus/cpu_arm.c:559,606; cpu_arm.h mode names</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — CONFIRMED — VERDICT: RATIFY WITH CHANGES (3).  All nine main-loop rulings UPHELD on the rules as written and the evidence re-checked: R1 member 3 at the complete set of guest CPSR.M writers (CPS decodes only as NOPs); R2 the backstops are reachable only after a debugger cpsr= plant; R3 the decline edits the local mask, never ic-&gt;arg[]; R4 no early return, new_cpsr used for the bank switch; R5 the probe AST-identical to all frozen copies (its own hash), 110 runs / 104 distinct / 103 red re-counted; R6 none of the final pass&#x27;s eight escapes is a FLOOR cell -- the five restore escapes act on bits 31:8 (single-operand marginals), the label x latch x bit residue is a disclosable two-axis joint, the two msr cells are label-conditioned where the switch lives in callees, M_msr_argmask adds a side effect and weakens no dominating predicate; the only breach was of the CONTRACT (clause e), repaired comment-only as (e) permits; R7 the stale dev_i80321.o scope; R8 the filed rows; R9 Astra&#x27;s corrections.  THREE CHANGES, applied before the commit: (1) Astra item 4 survived in the staged state_note -- corrected by the closing prefix; (2) the round&#x27;s records said Fable was not fired -- a sentence added to the CHANGELOG block, the commit message and the closure note; (3) PRETABLE5.out&#x27;s first-line mtime claim is void since its 08:13 append -- a dated clause in its CORRECTIONS.  Also taken: kimi-k3&#x27;s &#x27;new default: behaviour&#x27; ELSE checked FALSE (a CHANGELOG clause); gate_arm.sh&#x27;s `rows failed` grep is green only because note[:64] cuts the kill 14 note before &#x27; FAIL &#x27; (a latent FALSE RED, the safe direction) -&gt; armmsrmodedebt.  Noted for floodclass, not owed here: unlatched guest-drivable fatal()s in bdt_load (&#x27;s-bit: in usermode?&#x27;) and arm_exception (&#x27;Exception caused no mode change?&#x27;), and the latent ARM_EXCEPTION_TO_MODE[5] = 0. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/fable_adjudication_20261009/fable5_adjudication562.md; regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/fable_adjudication_20261009/FABLE_adjudication_brief_20261009.md — record: 2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  No panel was ever convened on this row.  The four recorded entries (deepseek, glm, grok, minimax) cite NO panel dir: they are remarks those seats volunteered about `armmsrmodekill` while answering some other round&#x27;s brief, recorded 2026-09-18 by the unread-seat sweep so the findings are not lost.  Recording them is what took this stage from ABSENT to 4/9 and reddened both stage gates -- the gate fired because the RECORD improved, not because a panel ran short.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — #562 the final pass (repair 5), the Opus 5.5 measuring seat (Agent tool; claude-opus-5-5 by agent_models.py): VERDICT: SHIP-WITH-CHANGES | 1 floor breach.  The breach is clause (e), the contract.  Product code is unchanged and still correct.  Codex&#x27;s prediction is CONFIRMED by measurement: a decline that forces N on the FIRST (latch-CLEAR) DPI or pop restore scores 47/47.  Two more edits do the same: OR-ing  Recorded seat by seat on 2026-10-09: the answer was read and adjudicated during the round (#562&#x27;s CHANGELOG block, the final pass (repair 5)) but had not been entered in the ledger. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/opus_pass2r5_20261009/opus_pass2r5.md</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — #562 the final pass (repair 5) -- this seat&#x27;s answer, verbatim excerpt: &quot;- Insert the section **before `gate_end`**. That function returns a status; appending checks afterward can overwrite the failure status and print the verdict prematurely.&quot;.  Recorded seat by seat on 2026-10-09: read and adjudicated during the round (#562&#x27;s CHANGELOG block, the final pass (repair 5)), not entered in the ledger until now. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r5_20261009/codex.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — #562 the final pass (repair 5) -- this seat&#x27;s answer, verbatim excerpt: &quot;VERDICT: SHIP-WITH-CHANGES | 0 floor breaches&quot;.  Recorded seat by seat on 2026-10-09: read and adjudicated during the round (#562&#x27;s CHANGELOG block, the final pass (repair 5)), not entered in the ledger until now. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r5_20261009/agy.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — #562 the final pass (repair 5) -- this seat&#x27;s answer, verbatim excerpt: &quot;I&#x27;ll review repair 5 against the working tree and the 47-row probe, and check the grid, the `ic-&gt;arg[]` claim, the records, and the gate wiring before any verdict.VERDICT: SHIP-WITH-CHANGES | 0 floor breaches&quot;.  Recorded seat by seat on 2026-10-09: read and adjudicated during the round (#562&#x27;s CHANGELOG block, the final pass (repair 5)), not entered in the ledger until now. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r5_20261009/grok.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — #562 the final pass (repair 5) -- this seat&#x27;s answer, verbatim excerpt: &quot;VERDICT: SHIP-WITH-CHANGES | 1 floor breach (the cached-translation cell, Q2); plus 2 records defects to fix in the same commit (Q3a, Q3b). Everything else I could check from the packet is internally consistent.&quot;.  Transport: kimi-k3:cloud (packet-fed); the kimi CLI returned 403 (&quot;subscription does not have access to Kimi Code&quot;) -- a SEAT FAILURE of that transport, not agreement (regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r5_20261009/kimi.txt).  Recorded seat by seat on 2026-10-09: read and adjudicated during the round (#562&#x27;s CHANGELOG block, the final pass (repair 5)), not entered in the ledger until now. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r5_20261009/ollama_kimi.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — #562 the final pass (repair 5) -- this seat&#x27;s answer, verbatim excerpt: &quot;VERDICT: SHIP-WITH-CHANGES | 2 floor breaches (+2 items not checkable from the packet, named below)&quot;.  Recorded seat by seat on 2026-10-09: read and adjudicated during the round (#562&#x27;s CHANGELOG block, the final pass (repair 5)), not entered in the ledger until now. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r5_20261009/ollama_glm.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — #562 the final pass (repair 5) -- this seat&#x27;s answer, verbatim excerpt: &quot;VERDICT: DO-NOT-SHIP | 1 floor breach&quot;.  Recorded seat by seat on 2026-10-09: read and adjudicated during the round (#562&#x27;s CHANGELOG block, the final pass (repair 5)), not entered in the ledger until now. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r5_20261009/ollama_deepseek.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — #562 the final pass (repair 5) -- this seat&#x27;s answer, verbatim excerpt: &quot;VERDICT: SHIP | 0 floor breaches&quot;.  Recorded seat by seat on 2026-10-09: read and adjudicated during the round (#562&#x27;s CHANGELOG block, the final pass (repair 5)), not entered in the ledger until now. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2r5_20261009/ollama_minimax.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-10-09 — CONFIRMED — VERDICT: REGRESS WITH FINDINGS (2) -- fresh builds of both shas in the seat&#x27;s own clone, ONE committed probe for both runs: parent 18/47 with the 29 named reds, commit 47/47, row-for-row and diag-for-diag IDENTICAL to the committed run_head.out / run_fixed.out; every red is `got=dead` on the death its row names; AST minus docstring 412a7157 for the committed probe and all three frozen copies.  Nothing landed that the review did not see.  Two L4 records corrections, both applied in the commit that recorded this entry: SHIPPED_vs_FROZEN.txt named the gate-TESTED blob 5ddc3242 where 59b99365 shipped (an UPDATE line appended), and armthumbabortkill&#x27;s &#x27;SA1110 has no Thumb (unchecked)&#x27; is answered by the tree. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/regress_20261009/fable5_regress562.md; regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/regress_20261009/probe_parent.out; regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/regress_20261009/probe_commit.out</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE PROBE WAS ATTACKED BEFORE IT WAS EVER RUN, AND IT HAD THREE INDEPENDENT DEFECTS. Verdict: &#x27;It runs.  It does not score.&#x27; ***  (1) **LINE LABELS ARE 16-BYTE ALIGNED** -- `debugger_cmds.c:709` is `addr = addr_start &amp; ~0xf`, so `dump 0x9004` labels the line `0x00009000` AND BLANK-PADS the leading word groups, shifting every group index.  (2) **THE DUMP RENDERS LITTLE-ENDIAN BYTES, NOT A WORD** -- 0x2a prints as `2a000000`.  (3) **`save 2s` READ A SLOT NOTHING WROTE**: its plant put SP at DEST+16 via `str_sp_10` while `classify()` read DEST+8, so ` sp` could NEVER appear and **the row was ALWAYS RED, pre-fix and post-fix alike** -- not a witness of Save at all. *** THE REMEDIES FOR (1) AND (2) ALREADY EXISTED IN THIS TREE AND I DID NOT USE THEM: `arm_bdt_probe.py:123-149` records the alignment measurement from 2026-09-11 in a seven-line comment, and `arm_flags_probe.py:191-193` carries `sw()` for the byte order. Knowledge present, unconsulted at the moment of use -- the carrier&#x27;s own recurring finding.  The repair COPIES both rather than inventing. *** **AND THE FAILURE MODE WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A FALSE GREEN, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  `live` wants 0x2a and would have got 0x2a000000; `armed` would have given `?/?`.  Both sit in the CONTROL=DEAD set, so the probe would have printed `ARMMSRMODE_CONTROL=DEAD`, `RESULT=0/0` and &#x27;SETUP FAILED -- NOT no defect&#x27;.  **The vehicle-kill design worked exactly as intended: it would have REFUSED TO SCORE rather than lying.**  That is the difference between a wasted run and a wrong record, and it is why the CONTROL=DEAD set is not decoration. *A self-check while repairing it showed why `arm_flags_probe.py` warns as it does: `deadbeef` read RAW yields 0xdeadbeef, so the POISON comparison would have MATCHED under the broken parser while every real value was wrong -- the sentinel&#x27;s own byte pattern hiding the defect.* **ONE CORRECTION RUNNING THE OTHER WAY, against the SPEC rather than the probe:** `print pc` wanting CODE+8 after `step 2` is RIGHT; the spec table&#x27;s CODE+4 is the wrong half, and a builder must not &#x27;fix&#x27; the probe down to it.  REPAIRED, AST clean, zero raw `dump` commands left.  **STILL NOT RUN** -- the adjudicated ship order puts the commit first, then this. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/PROBEATTACK_grok_20260917.txt; regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromcontractresidual</span><span class="ttl">gdrom contract: two same-commit L4s from the chain-closing pass were not applied -- a bare M3 label covering two different mutants, and an undated gdromdmasilent escape record</span><span class="tip">gdrom contract: two same-commit L4s from the chain-closing pass were not applied -- a bare M3 label covering two different mutants, and an undated gdromdmasilent escape record — witness: none -- documented-only.  Both items are RECORDS defects in a shipped contract block: no row, oracle, score or pin moves, which is verified rather than asserted (row-name set diffed against HEAD: identical). — note: [2026-10-02] A MEMBER OF `closeassessrows` (#557, from the census: regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/grok/grok_closedcensus.txt; confirmed regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/aconf_census/codex_aconf_census.txt).  Work still alive: the two contract corrections are NOT at HEAD (regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:56 bare M3, :119 the undated escape record) and the gdromdmasilent re-measurement is OWED (Astra, overturning grok).  The parked 2026-09-17 edit that carries them is NOT landed by #557: Astra&#x27;s review (aconf_parked, 2c) found it also adds two executable rows and tightens a third (54 -&gt; 56 row() sites; the gate pins 60 rows and the working probe makes 62), i.e. a detector change belonging to M2_gdromnodatawidth and gdromacceptdmanum, and its docstring contradicts the gdromdmasilent record in one place; it waits for a round of its own.  The row is NOT re-opened here: that decision is the class row&#x27;s work.  Filed rather than left implied.  (1) DESIGNED TO CATCH parenthesises a bare `M3&#x27; while old M3 relocates the clear past `break&#x27; and repair-B M3_gdromsuccesswidth is `if (len == 4)&#x27; at the success arm -- two mutants, one label.  (2) `gdromdmasilent&#x27; is listed as escaping every row, but arm bytetrig / nodata byte / accept byte all trigger with 0x5a and by reading would now red it; PREDICTED not measured, so DATE the escape record against the table it actually escaped rather than dropping it. *** BOTH L4s APPLIED 2026-09-17; ROW CLOSED AS A RECORDS FIX. ***  (1) the bare `M3&#x27; label is SPLIT into M3a (the original: relocates the shadow clear PAST `break&#x27;) and M3b (repair-B&#x27;s M3_gdromsuccesswidth: `if (len == 4)&#x27; at the SUCCESS arm, a WIDTH conditioning that reds `accept byte&#x27; alone at 61/62), with a note saying the MATRIX and not the bullet is the authority.  (2) the `gdromdmasilent&#x27; escape record is **DATED, NOT DROPPED** -- it escaped the table it was measured against, and the rows added since (`arm bytetrig&#x27;, `nodata byte&#x27;, `accept byte&#x27;) would BY READING red it now.  That is a PREDICTION, and dropping the line would have asserted a re-measurement nobody ran -- the frozen-number class wearing a tidy-up&#x27;s clothes.  The re-measurement is OWED and is named as such.  **VERIFIED AS RECORDS-ONLY, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS SENTENCE WAS FALSE.**  It read *&#x27;the row-name set is byte-identical to HEAD&#x27;s&#x27;*.  It is not: the diff against HEAD shows `accept byte&#x27; and `nodata byte&#x27; ADDED, because THIS SESSION&#x27;s clause-(b) repair added them and moved the gate pin 60 -&gt; 62.  The tree SHOULD differ from HEAD there. *** THE COMPARISON WAS SIMPLY THE WRONG ONE: a records-only claim has to answer &#x27;did MY EDIT move the row set?&#x27;, and I answered &#x27;does the TREE differ from HEAD?&#x27; -- a different question whose answer was always going to be yes, and whose counter-example my own guard printed while I read past it. ***  THE CORRECT GUARD, RUN: **56 `row(&#x27; call sites before and after the edit, indentation-tolerant -- unchanged**, both L4s land inside the module DOCSTRING, and the gate&#x27;s EQUALITY pin at 62 is untouched.  No kill table is perturbed. *Two further correlate slips in that same guard, recorded because they are this session&#x27;s pattern: my &#x27;row sites 54, was 56&#x27; alarm compared `^row\(&#x27; against an earlier `^\s*row\(&#x27; -- two REGEXES, not a lost row; and my &#x27;non-comment lines&#x27; count keyed on `#&#x27;, while the contract block is a docstring whose prose never starts with one.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED —  *** BOTH L4s APPLIED 2026-09-17; ROW CLOSED AS A RECORDS FIX. ***  (1) the bare `M3&#x27; label is SPLIT into M3a (the original: relocates the shadow clear PAST `break&#x27;) and M3b (repair-B&#x27;s M3_gdromsuccesswidth: `if (len == 4)&#x27; at the SUCCESS arm, a WIDTH conditioning that reds `accept byte&#x27; alone at 61/62), with a note saying the MATRIX and not the bullet is the authority.  (2) the `gdromdmasilent&#x27; escape record is **DATED, NOT DROPPED** -- it escaped the table it was measured against, and the rows added since (`arm bytetrig&#x27;, `nodata byte&#x27;, `accept byte&#x27;) would BY READING red it now.  That is a PREDICTION, and dropping the line would have asserted a re-measurement nobody ran -- the frozen-number class wearing a tidy-up&#x27;s clothes.  The re-measurement is OWED and is named as such.  **VERIFIED AS RECORDS-ONLY, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS SENTENCE WAS FALSE.**  It read *&#x27;the row-name set is byte-identical to HEAD&#x27;s&#x27;*.  It is not: the diff against HEAD shows `accept byte&#x27; and `nodata byte&#x27; ADDED, because THIS SESSION&#x27;s clause-(b) repair added them and moved the gate pin 60 -&gt; 62.  The tree SHOULD differ from HEAD there. *** THE COMPARISON WAS SIMPLY THE WRONG ONE: a records-only claim has to answer &#x27;did MY EDIT move the row set?&#x27;, and I answered &#x27;does the TREE differ from HEAD?&#x27; -- a different question whose answer was always going to be yes, and whose counter-example my own guard printed while I read past it. ***  THE CORRECT GUARD, RUN: **56 `row(&#x27; call sites before and after the edit, indentation-tolerant -- unchanged**, both L4s land inside the module DOCSTRING, and the gate&#x27;s EQUALITY pin at 62 is untouched.  No kill table is perturbed. *Two further correlate slips in that same guard, recorded because they are this session&#x27;s pattern: my &#x27;row sites 54, was 56&#x27; alarm compared `^row\(&#x27; against an earlier `^\s*row\(&#x27; -- two REGEXES, not a lost row; and my &#x27;non-comment lines&#x27; count keyed on `#&#x27;, while the contract block is a docstring whose prose never starts with one.* — evidence: regress/gdrom_default_probe.py contract block; regress/gate_sh_rounding.sh pin unchanged at 62</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">highrankunmeasured</span><span class="ttl">the order between the two open HIGHs rests on real-guest reachability measured for neither -- ip30&#x27;s aborts are on no in-tree path, and armmsr&#x27;s probe is built but unrun</span><span class="tip">the order between the two open HIGHs rests on real-guest reachability measured for neither -- ip30&#x27;s aborts are on no in-tree path, and armmsr&#x27;s probe is built but unrun — witness: none -- documented-only.  ANSWERED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR on both sides: armmsr&#x27;s reachability by mode enumeration (7 handled, 25 unhandled, none producible by a correct in-tree kernel), and ip30&#x27;s by gate_asan_sweep.sh:593 running ip30 green.  The ruling is ip30abortkill first. — note: *** BOTH OPEN HIGHs&#x27; SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device header says it is &#x27;just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.&#x27;  The defect is real for a guest that touches those addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** BOTH OPEN HIGHs&#x27; SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device header says it is &#x27;just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.&#x27;  The defect is real for a guest that touches those addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently. — evidence: gate_asan_sweep.sh:593; regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py (built, unrun); ledger witness fields</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).** — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/REACH_grok_20260917.txt; src/cpus/cpu_arm.c:559,606; cpu_arm.h mode names</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">probedeathoracle</span><span class="ttl">census of probes that could report `alive&#x27; for a process that already died -- 0 shipped live probes mis-score; the class was born and closed the same day</span><span class="tip">census of probes that could report `alive&#x27; for a process that already died -- 0 shipped live probes mis-score; the class was born and closed the same day — witness: none -- documented-only, and the CLOSURE IS THE RESULT: a census with its denominator (88 live launchers, 5 with WNOHANG, 1 sampling once, 0 using waitpid-once as the survive oracle) bounding a class rather than leaving it open. — note: *** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 AND THE CLASS IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: **0 shipped live probes still mis-score this way.**  The defect was born today and died today. ***  The founding case is `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py`, which had the exact shape, was MEASURED reporting `alive` for a process that had SIGABRT&#x27;d, and was rewritten to poll the same hour.  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` was built with the polling form from the start. **METHOD, keyed on MECHANISM rather than on names** -- and it explicitly refused to count `artifacts/**` frozen copies, which would have inflated the class about FIFTEENFOLD by re-counting the same five files: live population is `regress/*.py` = **99 files**, minus 10 harness/selftest/census scripts and one source-only probe = **88 launchers**. **THE DEATH-AS-ORACLE FAMILIES, with counts:** EOF-as-alive (`alive = rd(...)`) **33**; sessions borrowed from those 33 **11** (pcc2_default execs pcc2_width&#x27;s session; ten SH4 probes import sh4_pcic_probe); prompt/dump still-answering **3**; `subprocess.run` wait-status **6**; **`WNOHANG` at all: 5** (4 at HEAD plus the uncommitted arm_msrmode). **OF THOSE FIVE, HOW MANY SAMPLE waitpid ONCE?**  `pcc2_width_probe.py` polls 30x0.1s and waitpid only fills `how`; `pcc2_c040_probe.py` the same; `xscale_inten_probe.py` polls 2s in `close()` and sets `self.dead` on EOF during `send()`; `arm_msrmode_probe.py` polls 12s. `luna_absentcpu_probe.py` **does sample once -- and it is NOT the alive oracle**, being called from `died()` only after `step_at` has already failed on EOF.  **ONCE-ONLY IN THE LIVE TREE: 1.  WAITPID-ONCE AS THE SURVIVE ORACLE: 0.** *** AND THE SEAT NAMED THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN THE KEYWORD, which is what makes this reusable: the bug is NOT &#x27;has WNOHANG&#x27;.  It is the three-part combination -- `rd()` returns on an empty `os.read` so the spin is skipped exactly on death; that return is IGNORED; and `alive` is then taken from a single `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.  Any one of the three alone is harmless. ***  **DISPOSITION: NO TREE-WIDE EDIT.**  The seat&#x27;s own words -- *&#x27;a tree-wide rewrite would manufacture findings&#x27;* -- and this project&#x27;s addition licence agree: 33 EOF-as-alive probes consume `rd()`&#x27;s return and are correct as written.  The boundary is recorded so a future round does not rediscover the class and sweep it.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 AND THE CLASS IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: **0 shipped live probes still mis-score this way.**  The defect was born today and died today. ***  The founding case is `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py`, which had the exact shape, was MEASURED reporting `alive` for a process that had SIGABRT&#x27;d, and was rewritten to poll the same hour.  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` was built with the polling form from the start. **METHOD, keyed on MECHANISM rather than on names** -- and it explicitly refused to count `artifacts/**` frozen copies, which would have inflated the class about FIFTEENFOLD by re-counting the same five files: live population is `regress/*.py` = **99 files**, minus 10 harness/selftest/census scripts and one source-only probe = **88 launchers**. **THE DEATH-AS-ORACLE FAMILIES, with counts:** EOF-as-alive (`alive = rd(...)`) **33**; sessions borrowed from those 33 **11** (pcc2_default execs pcc2_width&#x27;s session; ten SH4 probes import sh4_pcic_probe); prompt/dump still-answering **3**; `subprocess.run` wait-status **6**; **`WNOHANG` at all: 5** (4 at HEAD plus the uncommitted arm_msrmode). **OF THOSE FIVE, HOW MANY SAMPLE waitpid ONCE?**  `pcc2_width_probe.py` polls 30x0.1s and waitpid only fills `how`; `pcc2_c040_probe.py` the same; `xscale_inten_probe.py` polls 2s in `close()` and sets `self.dead` on EOF during `send()`; `arm_msrmode_probe.py` polls 12s. `luna_absentcpu_probe.py` **does sample once -- and it is NOT the alive oracle**, being called from `died()` only after `step_at` has already failed on EOF.  **ONCE-ONLY IN THE LIVE TREE: 1.  WAITPID-ONCE AS THE SURVIVE ORACLE: 0.** *** AND THE SEAT NAMED THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN THE KEYWORD, which is what makes this reusable: the bug is NOT &#x27;has WNOHANG&#x27;.  It is the three-part combination -- `rd()` returns on an empty `os.read` so the spin is skipped exactly on death; that return is IGNORED; and `alive` is then taken from a single `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.  Any one of the three alone is harmless. ***  **DISPOSITION: NO TREE-WIDE EDIT.**  The seat&#x27;s own words -- *&#x27;a tree-wide rewrite would manufacture findings&#x27;* -- and this project&#x27;s addition licence agree: 33 EOF-as-alive probes consume `rd()`&#x27;s return and are correct as written.  The boundary is recorded so a future round does not rediscover the class and sweep it. — evidence: regress/artifacts/probedeathoracle_20260917/CENSUS_grok_20260917.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">grokampkill</span><span class="ttl">three grok seats lost to a trailing &amp; inside a Bash-tool wrapper -- the documented hazard, hit after nine correct launches, detected by MTIME rather than by size or process count</span><span class="tip">three grok seats lost to a trailing &amp; inside a Bash-tool wrapper -- the documented hazard, hit after nine correct launches, detected by MTIME rather than by size or process count — witness: harness:mtime on the redirect target -- 3 of 5 jobs unwritten for 338-675 s while 2 were written within 80 s, plus a partial ending mid-sentence with no verdict line. — note: *** THREE GROK SEATS WERE LOST TO THE DOCUMENTED PLAIN-`&amp;` HAZARD ON 2026-09-17, AND THE CARRIER DESCRIBES IT EXACTLY. ***  A trailing `&amp;` inside a Bash-tool invocation does not survive: the child is torn down when that invocation&#x27;s shell exits.  I used the correct form -- the grok command in the FOREGROUND of a `run_in_background` wrapper -- for the first nine jobs, then broke it on the tenth by trying to fire TWO seats from ONE wrapper with `&amp;`.  **Knowing the rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems**, which is the carrier&#x27;s own structure-versus-memory thesis arriving against me. **HOW IT WAS DETECTED, because the obvious check is the wrong one:** the files were SMALL but not empty, which the carrier warns is indistinguishable between a stall, a wall and a dead run.  `tasklist` showed 3 live grok processes against 5 jobs, which is suggestive and not decisive because grok spawns more than one process per job.  **The decisive observable was MTIME**: `allocsize` and `records` had been written 61 s and 80 s earlier (alive), while `lunadefault`, `maplebound` and `cpusweep` had not been touched for 338-675 s.  A killed job&#x27;s redirect target stops growing; a live one does not. **AND THE TAIL CONFIRMED IT**: `grok_lunadefault.txt` ended mid-sentence in streaming reasoning -- *&#x27;next I will list every exact `case` versus the board map&#x27;* -- with no verdict line.  That is a STOPPED SEAT, and the standing rule is that **a stopped seat is a SEAT FAILURE, never agreement**: re-fire once, preserve the partial rather than deleting it. All three partials are preserved as `grok_&lt;job&gt;.STOPPED_partial.txt` and all three were re-fired one per wrapper, command in the foreground. *Two jobs fired the same way SURVIVED (`allocsize`, `records`), which is worth recording because it makes the hazard INTERMITTENT rather than deterministic -- and an intermittent failure that leaves a plausible-looking partial is exactly the shape this project keeps misfiling as a seat problem rather than a launcher one.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THREE GROK SEATS WERE LOST TO THE DOCUMENTED PLAIN-`&amp;` HAZARD ON 2026-09-17, AND THE CARRIER DESCRIBES IT EXACTLY. ***  A trailing `&amp;` inside a Bash-tool invocation does not survive: the child is torn down when that invocation&#x27;s shell exits.  I used the correct form -- the grok command in the FOREGROUND of a `run_in_background` wrapper -- for the first nine jobs, then broke it on the tenth by trying to fire TWO seats from ONE wrapper with `&amp;`.  **Knowing the rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems**, which is the carrier&#x27;s own structure-versus-memory thesis arriving against me. **HOW IT WAS DETECTED, because the obvious check is the wrong one:** the files were SMALL but not empty, which the carrier warns is indistinguishable between a stall, a wall and a dead run.  `tasklist` showed 3 live grok processes against 5 jobs, which is suggestive and not decisive because grok spawns more than one process per job.  **The decisive observable was MTIME**: `allocsize` and `records` had been written 61 s and 80 s earlier (alive), while `lunadefault`, `maplebound` and `cpusweep` had not been touched for 338-675 s.  A killed job&#x27;s redirect target stops growing; a live one does not. **AND THE TAIL CONFIRMED IT**: `grok_lunadefault.txt` ended mid-sentence in streaming reasoning -- *&#x27;next I will list every exact `case` versus the board map&#x27;* -- with no verdict line.  That is a STOPPED SEAT, and the standing rule is that **a stopped seat is a SEAT FAILURE, never agreement**: re-fire once, preserve the partial rather than deleting it. All three partials are preserved as `grok_&lt;job&gt;.STOPPED_partial.txt` and all three were re-fired one per wrapper, command in the foreground. *Two jobs fired the same way SURVIVED (`allocsize`, `records`), which is worth recording because it makes the hazard INTERMITTENT rather than deterministic -- and an intermittent failure that leaves a plausible-looking partial is exactly the shape this project keeps misfiling as a seat problem rather than a launcher one.* — evidence: _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_lunadefault.STOPPED_partial.txt _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_maplebound.STOPPED_partial.txt _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_cpusweep.STOPPED_partial.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">scorehyphenfp</span><span class="ttl">check_score_claims.py read the ordinal pair `entries-4/8` as a score because its lookbehind excluded a digit, x and / but not a hyphen -- and its prescribed remedy would have written a false statement into a probe</span><span class="tip">check_score_claims.py read the ordinal pair `entries-4/8` as a score because its lookbehind excluded a digit, x and / but not a hyphen -- and its prescribed remedy would have written a false statement into a probe — commits: 7440e129 — witness: harness:check_score_claims.py controlled in both directions -- 8 real score claims still match, 6 non-scores rejected; SCORE_CLAIMS_PASS at 30 pinned probes — note: [2026-10-02] COMMITS FIELD FILLED by #557 from the census of closed rows with no commit (regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/grok/grok_closedcensus.txt; Astra&#x27;s git log, regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/aconf_census/codex_aconf_census.txt): 7440e129 -- #529&#x27;s commit; touches tools/pipeline/check_score_claims.py.  *** FIXED 2026-09-17 BY OWNER DECISION, WITH A SIBLING AUDIT RATHER THAN A ONE-INSTANCE PATCH. ***  `check_score_claims.py`&#x27;s `SCORE` regex was `(?&lt;![\dx/])(\d+)\s*/\s*(\d+)(?![\d/%])` -- a lookbehind excluding a digit, `x` and `/` **but not a hyphen** -- so the ORDINAL PAIR `entries-4/8` was reported as a stale score claim of 4/8 against a pin of 188. *** THE REASON THIS WAS NOT LEFT ALONE: THE CHECKER&#x27;S OWN PRESCRIBED REMEDY WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A FALSE STATEMENT. ***  Its message reads *&quot;if that is a deliberate historical quote, write &#x27;8-row&#x27; on the same line&quot;* -- and on a line about ARRAY ENTRIES that silences the check by writing something untrue into a probe.  **A check whose repair corrupts the thing it guards** is the shape the carrier already records for the build-tree clearance recipe, arrived at from a new direction. **THE AUDIT, with a denominator, because a correlate-keyed matcher usually has more than one victim:** 710 N/M matches across 121 `regress/` files; the hyphen-aware form keeps **706** and drops exactly **FOUR**, every one an ordinal pair and none a score -- `entries-4/8` (`crime_width_probe.py`), `len-3/5` (`lca_isalane_probe.py` comment 96), `opcode-59/63` (`ppc_halt_probe.py` comment 238), `pass 2-3 / 6-7` (`sgi_de_primop_probe.py` comment 985).  *** THREE OF THE FOUR WERE LATENT: they fire only once their probe gains a gate pin, so the class had four members and one visible face. *** **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BEFORE LANDING:** eight real score claims still match (13/13, 26/26, 8/32, `52 / 52`, 16/16, `scored 27/29`, `base 29/29`, `RESULT=6/9`); six non-scores rejected (the four hyphen pairs plus the two pre-existing hex lists).  `SCORE_CLAIMS_PASS` at 30 pinned probes. *The fix is one character in a lookbehind.  What made it worth a round is that the audit converted an anecdote into a population -- and the population was four.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** FIXED 2026-09-17 BY OWNER DECISION, WITH A SIBLING AUDIT RATHER THAN A ONE-INSTANCE PATCH. ***  `check_score_claims.py`&#x27;s `SCORE` regex was `(?&lt;![\dx/])(\d+)\s*/\s*(\d+)(?![\d/%])` -- a lookbehind excluding a digit, `x` and `/` **but not a hyphen** -- so the ORDINAL PAIR `entries-4/8` was reported as a stale score claim of 4/8 against a pin of 188. *** THE REASON THIS WAS NOT LEFT ALONE: THE CHECKER&#x27;S OWN PRESCRIBED REMEDY WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A FALSE STATEMENT. ***  Its message reads *&quot;if that is a deliberate historical quote, write &#x27;8-row&#x27; on the same line&quot;* -- and on a line about ARRAY ENTRIES that silences the check by writing something untrue into a probe.  **A check whose repair corrupts the thing it guards** is the shape the carrier already records for the build-tree clearance recipe, arrived at from a new direction. **THE AUDIT, with a denominator, because a correlate-keyed matcher usually has more than one victim:** 710 N/M matches across 121 `regress/` files; the hyphen-aware form keeps **706** and drops exactly **FOUR**, every one an ordinal pair and none a score -- `entries-4/8` (`crime_width_probe.py`), `len-3/5` (`lca_isalane_probe.py` comment 96), `opcode-59/63` (`ppc_halt_probe.py` comment 238), `pass 2-3 / 6-7` (`sgi_de_primop_probe.py` comment 985).  *** THREE OF THE FOUR WERE LATENT: they fire only once their probe gains a gate pin, so the class had four members and one visible face. *** **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BEFORE LANDING:** eight real score claims still match (13/13, 26/26, 8/32, `52 / 52`, 16/16, `scored 27/29`, `base 29/29`, `RESULT=6/9`); six non-scores rejected (the four hyphen pairs plus the two pre-existing hex lists).  `SCORE_CLAIMS_PASS` at 30 pinned probes. *The fix is one character in a lookbehind.  What made it worth a round is that the audit converted an anecdote into a population -- and the population was four.* — evidence: tools/pipeline/check_score_claims.py; 710-match sibling audit over regress/*.py and *.sh</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c hold"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">⏸</span><span class="tip">2026-10-02 — awaiting fable5 — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5] scorehyphenfp closed at 7440e129 and its commits field stayed EMPTY until #557 filled it, so section I never saw the row: the REGRESS review -- a distinct phase under the regress-is-fable-only directive -- was neither done nor held.  Held now by #557 (2026-10-02); the Fable agent seat is deliberately not fired (owner instruction, 2026-10-02), so the hold stands for the owner&#x27;s batched flagship sign-off.  The question is in the row&#x27;s hold_question.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">boilerplateprefix</span><span class="ttl">check_boilerplate.py keyed on note[:60], so it clustered on the opening sentence -- 20 of 64 clusters were false positives AND it hid ten genuine stamps by merging them into mixed buckets</span><span class="tip">check_boilerplate.py keyed on note[:60], so it clustered on the opening sentence -- 20 of 64 clusters were false positives AND it hid ten genuine stamps by merging them into mixed buckets — commits: e1871fa5 — witness: harness:check_boilerplate.py controlled in both directions -- BOILERPLATE_PASS with 7 false positives cleared and 54 genuine stamps retained; an injected 4-seat identical note post-cutoff reds it and names the row; removal restores green — note: [2026-10-02] the repair (the full-note comparison key) sat UNCOMMITTED in the working tree from 2026-09-17 while this row read closed; the census found it (Astra, overturning grok, who had read the working copy).  #557 commits it, with its comment corrected per Astra&#x27;s review (the preamble is 81 characters, not 66; the landing language; the recall overstatement; the stale header) and its controls re-run on the day (regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/draft/boilerplate_controls_20261002.out); the commits field is filled with #557&#x27;s sha by its post step.  *** `check_boilerplate.py` COMPARED THE FIRST 60 CHARACTERS OF A SEAT NOTE, SO IT CLUSTERED ON THE OPENING SENTENCE RATHER THAN ON THE NOTE.  Fixed 2026-09-17 by owner decision, with the audit run first. ***  Cells that carried each seat&#x27;s OWN finding but shared an identifying preamble -- `LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431).` is 66 characters before the first seat writes a word -- were reported as boilerplate **while satisfying this check&#x27;s own stated rule**, which asks for *&quot;each seat its own sentence from its own file&quot;*.  They had their own sentence. **MEASURED OVER EVERY CLUSTER IN THE LEDGER, BOTH KEYS, SAME RUN:**  OLD `note[:60]` -- 64 clusters, 44 genuine, **20 NOTES DIFFER**; NEW full note -- 54 clusters, **54 genuine, 0 notes differ**. *** IT FAILED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, AND THE SECOND HALF IS THE SURPRISE: GENUINE STAMPS ROSE 44 -&gt; 54. ***  A 60-character bucket holding a real stamp ALONGSIDE other notes scored as one mixed cluster, so **the prefix key was HIDING TEN REAL STAMPS BY MERGING THEM**.  Truncation cost recall as well as precision; it never bought either. **ALL SEVEN LIVE REDS WERE FALSE**, each with seven genuinely distinct notes -- so section O was blocking commits on nothing. **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:** `BOILERPLATE_PASS` with the seven gone; an INJECTED identical paragraph across four seat cells post-cutoff turns it red and names the row; removing the injection restores green. *** AND A HARNESS BUG OF MINE IS RECORDED IN THE FILE, because it produced a wrong number first: `shared_notes` returns its key with NEWLINES REPLACED BY SPACES for display, so a verification that compares a recomputed key against the RETURNED one silently matches nothing for any note containing a newline -- and then reports the cluster as non-identical. ***  It made one cluster (`wdcnoirq`) look like a survivor when the fix had worked.  *Recompute the bucket; never compare against the display key.*  **Third time this session that a check of mine keyed on a correlate of the thing rather than the thing.**</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phla